WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.961 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.961 --> 00:00:03.909 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:09.050 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.800 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So today
we're going to continue

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.380 align:middle line:90%
with our study of auctions.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.440 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
study a bit of a richer

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.600 align:middle line:84%
model than the example
that we looked at so far.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.560 align:middle line:84%
But let's just recall what
we've done so far with auctions.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.640 align:middle line:84%
So far, we looked at
this very simple example

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.480 align:middle line:84%
where we just had two bidders,
and each bidder's valuation

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.520 align:middle line:84%
for the item was
uniformly distributed

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.180 align:middle line:90%
on the 0, 1 interval.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.720 align:middle line:90%
So let's look at this example.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:43.040 align:middle line:84%
We had our example where
we had two bidders,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:57.680 align:middle line:84%
and their valuations v1 and v2
were independent and uniform

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.940 align:middle line:90%
on the interval 0, 1.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.620 align:middle line:84%
And we looked at two
different auction

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.300 align:middle line:90%
formats in this simple example.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.760 align:middle line:84%
The first auction format would
be a first-price auction.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:16.260 align:middle line:90%


00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:17.960 align:middle line:84%
Remember, in a
first-price auction,

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.900 align:middle line:90%
each bidder submits a bid.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.500 align:middle line:84%
The person who submits a
higher bid wins the item.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.638 align:middle line:84%
And the amount that they pay
is the amount of their bid.

00:01:26.638 --> 00:01:29.180 align:middle line:84%
And what we showed is that in
a first-price auction, at least

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.940 align:middle line:84%
in this example, we
had an equilibrium

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:36.340 align:middle line:84%
where each bidder bid exactly
half of her valuation.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:41.380 align:middle line:84%
So we had an equilibrium
where maybe b1 star of v1

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:50.020 align:middle line:84%
equals v1 over m and b2
star of v2 equals v2 over 2.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.780 align:middle line:84%
So this was a
symmetric equilibrium.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.940 align:middle line:84%
Each bidder bids exactly the
same way, given their valuation

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:57.520 align:middle line:90%
evaluation.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.680 align:middle line:84%
And of course, we noticed
that each bidder is always

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.620 align:middle line:84%
going to bid strictly less
than their true valuation.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.180 align:middle line:84%
If they bid their
true valuation,

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:06.680 align:middle line:84%
well, they can never
come out ahead,

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.303 align:middle line:84%
because even if they
won the auction,

00:02:08.303 --> 00:02:10.720 align:middle line:84%
they'd end up paying exactly
what they value the good for.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.480 align:middle line:84%
So this way, there's a
chance that when they win--

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.000 align:middle line:84%
or when they do
win, they're always

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.520 align:middle line:84%
paying strictly less than
their valuation for the good.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.660 align:middle line:84%
Another auction you could run
is a second-price auction.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:27.360 align:middle line:90%


00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.520 align:middle line:84%
And in a second-price auction,
the bidders submit their bids,

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.360 align:middle line:84%
just like in a
first-price auction,

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.560 align:middle line:84%
and the rule for
allocating the good

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.540 align:middle line:84%
is the same as in a
first-price auction.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.960 align:middle line:84%
The person who bids
the most gets the item.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.000 align:middle line:84%
The difference from
a first-price auction

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.320 align:middle line:90%
is the payment rule.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.560 align:middle line:84%
So in a second-price
auction, the winning bidder

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.280 align:middle line:84%
doesn't pay their
own bid, but they

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.600 align:middle line:84%
pay the second-highest
bid-- that is the highest

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.240 align:middle line:90%
bid among everyone else.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.020 align:middle line:84%
And we argued informally that
in the second-price auction,

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.550 align:middle line:84%
it's optimal to bid
your true valuation.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:03.610 align:middle line:84%
So here, we have an equilibrium
where b1 star of b1 equals v1,

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:07.650 align:middle line:90%
and b2 star of v2 equals v2.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.850 align:middle line:84%
And I want to start by arguing
why this is equilibrium, why you

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:14.450 align:middle line:84%
always want to bid exactly
your true valuation

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:15.670 align:middle line:90%
in a second-price auction.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:18.690 align:middle line:90%


00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:22.050 align:middle line:84%
So to see this, let's consider
a Second-Price Auction,

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:26.110 align:middle line:84%
SPA, and let's take the
perspective of bidder 1.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:28.770 align:middle line:90%


00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.690 align:middle line:84%
So suppose I'm bidder 1,
and suppose my valuation

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:32.990 align:middle line:90%
for the good is v1.

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:38.650 align:middle line:90%


00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:42.290 align:middle line:84%
And we want to look at
different ways I could bid.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.130 align:middle line:84%
So we want to argue
that it's always better

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:48.130 align:middle line:84%
to bid my true valuation v1
than to make a different bid.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:49.670 align:middle line:84%
Well, how could I
bid differently?

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.930 align:middle line:84%
Well, there's two rough ways
I could bid differently.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.750 align:middle line:84%
I could either bid
more than my valuation

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.170 align:middle line:90%
or less than my valuation.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:58.870 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to analyze
those two cases separately.

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:05.190 align:middle line:84%
So let's think about
our bid space here.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.110 align:middle line:90%
So this is 0.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:12.830 align:middle line:84%
And let's fix our
valuation here, v1.

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:14.390 align:middle line:84%
And we want to
first see, well what

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:17.149 align:middle line:84%
if instead of bidding
my true valuation v1,

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:24.790 align:middle line:84%
I chose an alternative bid,
b1 prime that was, say, here.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:30.390 align:middle line:84%
So I'm contemplating a deviation
where instead of bidding v1,

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:31.950 align:middle line:90%
I bid b1 prime.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.790 align:middle line:84%
So I bid strictly higher
than my true valuation.

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:36.910 align:middle line:84%
And we want to argue that
this can never help me,

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:40.110 align:middle line:84%
that I'm never better
off by bidding b1

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.710 align:middle line:90%
prime than I am bidding v1.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:45.350 align:middle line:84%
And to see this, well, what
happens in this auction

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.190 align:middle line:84%
is going to depend on what
the other bidder bids.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:52.570 align:middle line:84%
So I want to think of this
axis here as representing what

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:54.390 align:middle line:90%
the other bidder is bidding.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:57.050 align:middle line:84%
And then I want to compare
the performance of this bid

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:01.130 align:middle line:84%
to this bid for each possible
way that the other player bids.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:03.250 align:middle line:90%
So let's first start down here.

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:06.770 align:middle line:84%
Suppose the other player
b2, the other player

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:09.210 align:middle line:84%
makes a bid b2
that is, down here.

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.450 align:middle line:90%
It's strictly smaller than v1.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.570 align:middle line:90%
OK, so what happens if I bid v1?

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:18.970 align:middle line:84%
And what happens
if I bid b1 prime?

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:19.510 align:middle line:90%
Let's start.

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:22.710 align:middle line:84%
What if I bid v1 and the
other player bids b2?

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:23.592 align:middle line:90%
What happens?

00:05:23.592 --> 00:05:25.550 align:middle line:84%
What's the outcome of
the second-price auction?

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:26.690 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, in the front?

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:28.250 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: You pay b2, right?

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:29.730 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: You pay
b2, and you win.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:30.930 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.370 align:middle line:90%
And you win the good.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:35.650 align:middle line:84%
What if you had
instead bid b1 prime?

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:36.290 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:36.870 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The same thing.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:37.828 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Same outcome.

00:05:37.828 --> 00:05:40.150 align:middle line:84%
So if the opponent's
bid is down here,

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:43.590 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't make a difference
whether I bid v1 or b1 prime.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:47.890 align:middle line:84%
Either way, I win the
item, and I pay the bid b2.

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:54.870 align:middle line:84%
So here, we'll say no difference
between v1 and b1 prime.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:56.330 align:middle line:90%
What if b2 is up here?

00:05:56.330 --> 00:06:00.190 align:middle line:90%


00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:04.230 align:middle line:84%
Now what happens if I bid
v1, and if I bid b1 prime?

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:04.870 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:05.790 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: You lose either way.

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:07.040 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: You lose either way.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:09.270 align:middle line:84%
So here, it also doesn't
make a difference.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:13.292 align:middle line:84%
They're outbidding me whether I
bid b1 prime or v1, so I lose.

00:06:13.292 --> 00:06:15.250 align:middle line:84%
I don't get the item,
and I don't pay anything,

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:16.333 align:middle line:90%
so it makes no difference.

00:06:16.333 --> 00:06:20.550 align:middle line:90%


00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.610 align:middle line:90%
What if they bid in here?

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:27.950 align:middle line:90%


00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:30.870 align:middle line:84%
And we could worry about them
bidding exactly V1 or exactly b1

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.010 align:middle line:84%
prime, but that just
adds some complications.

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:36.110 align:middle line:84%
So let's say it's strictly
between v1 and b1 prime.

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:39.150 align:middle line:90%
Now what happens if I bid v1?

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:44.670 align:middle line:84%
I lose, because I've been outbid
by my opponent, so I get 0.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:48.610 align:middle line:84%
If I instead bid b1 prime,
well, this sounds pretty good,

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:49.670 align:middle line:90%
because now I win.

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:51.170 align:middle line:90%
I win the auction.

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:53.970 align:middle line:84%
But what happens if I
win by bidding b1 prime

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.730 align:middle line:90%
when my opponent bids b2 here?

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:57.450 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.062 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You're paying more
than your actual valuation.

00:07:00.062 --> 00:07:01.770 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So I'm
actually worse off here,

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:05.770 align:middle line:84%
because I could bid
my true valuation v1,

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:08.970 align:middle line:84%
pay nothing, and win
nothing, and my payoff is 0.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:12.850 align:middle line:84%
Or if I bid b1 prime,
I outbid my opponent.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:16.130 align:middle line:84%
I win the item, but now I have
to pay the second-highest bid,

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:17.690 align:middle line:90%
which in this case is b2.

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:20.550 align:middle line:84%
So I'm paying more for the item
than it's actually worth to me.

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:24.070 align:middle line:84%
So I'm strictly worse off
by bidding b1 prime than v1.

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:28.130 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, I'll say
v1 is strictly preferred--

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:30.290 align:middle line:90%
this is kind of a squiggly--

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:32.330 align:middle line:90%
to b1 prime.

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:34.390 align:middle line:84%
So there's three separate
cases we've analyzed.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:36.090 align:middle line:84%
In two of the cases,
it doesn't matter

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.250 align:middle line:90%
whether I bid v1 or b1 prime.

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:40.530 align:middle line:84%
And in one of the
cases I strictly prefer

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:43.890 align:middle line:84%
bidding my true valuation
of v1 to bidding b1 prime.

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:46.620 align:middle line:84%
So the argument is
almost done, but we only

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:49.348 align:middle line:84%
considered bids strictly
higher than my valuation.

00:07:49.348 --> 00:07:51.140 align:middle line:84%
We now have to go
through the same argument

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:53.260 align:middle line:84%
and make sure that I
never want to bid strictly

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.280 align:middle line:90%
less than my valuation.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:58.220 align:middle line:84%
So let's go through the
same thing over here.

00:07:58.220 --> 00:07:59.580 align:middle line:90%
So same figure.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:04.860 align:middle line:84%
Here's v1, but now we're going
to contemplate a downward bid.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.100 align:middle line:84%
And you might think this
is the more common bid when

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:09.180 align:middle line:84%
we played the-- or the
more common mistake.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.460 align:middle line:84%
When we played this first-price
or the second-price auction,

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:14.720 align:middle line:84%
some people-- actually,
most people bid pretty well,

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:17.960 align:middle line:84%
but some people bid strictly
less than their valuation.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:20.320 align:middle line:84%
So let's see whether
this is a good idea.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:24.272 align:middle line:84%
And let's again analyze
our three cases.

00:08:24.272 --> 00:08:25.480 align:middle line:90%
And again, I say three cases.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:27.563 align:middle line:84%
We have to be a little
careful if it's right here,

00:08:27.563 --> 00:08:29.400 align:middle line:84%
but it's not going
to change anything.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:33.179 align:middle line:90%


00:08:33.179 --> 00:08:37.580 align:middle line:84%
So case 1, what if my
opponent's bid is down here?

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:39.500 align:middle line:84%
It's strictly
lower than b1 prime

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:41.299 align:middle line:90%
and it's strictly lower than v1?

00:08:41.299 --> 00:08:42.700 align:middle line:90%
Well, same thing.

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:45.600 align:middle line:84%
I lose either way--
sorry, I win either way.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.880 align:middle line:84%
And I pay b2 either way,
so there's no difference.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:54.080 align:middle line:90%


00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.520 align:middle line:90%
Up here, well, I'm being outbid.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:00.320 align:middle line:84%
b2 is strictly higher than v1,
and it's strictly higher than b1

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:01.000 align:middle line:90%
prime.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:04.120 align:middle line:84%
So whether I bid my true
valuation or b1 prime,

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:05.480 align:middle line:90%
I lose the auction.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:07.720 align:middle line:90%
I pay nothing, I get nothing.

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:09.580 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, it
makes no difference.

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:12.160 align:middle line:90%


00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:16.440 align:middle line:84%
But again, the critical
region is this middle region,

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:20.560 align:middle line:84%
where my opponent's bid
b2 is strictly between b1

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:22.080 align:middle line:90%
prime and v1.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.460 align:middle line:84%
So what happens here if I
bid truthfully and bid v1?

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:30.600 align:middle line:90%


00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:33.265 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: You win it and pay b2.

00:09:33.265 --> 00:09:33.932 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:09:33.932 --> 00:09:36.720 align:middle line:84%
And what if I had
instead bid b1 prime?

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:37.920 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: You'd lose.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:38.712 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: I'd lose.

00:09:38.712 --> 00:09:41.000 align:middle line:84%
So I'm better off
bidding truthfully,

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:43.800 align:middle line:84%
because I win and pay
less than my valuation,

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:45.780 align:middle line:84%
so I make a strictly
positive utility.

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:48.600 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if I had deviated
and bid b1 prime,

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:51.720 align:middle line:84%
I would have lost the auction,
won nothing, paid nothing,

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:53.240 align:middle line:84%
and therefore got
a utility of 0.

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:59.820 align:middle line:84%
So once again, we see that v1 is
strictly better than b1 prime.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:02.140 align:middle line:84%
So now we've completed
our graphical argument

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:06.860 align:middle line:84%
to show that bidding truthfully
is better than bidding anything

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:07.580 align:middle line:90%
else.

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:10.020 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, what we've shown
is that bidding truthfully

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:13.260 align:middle line:84%
is actually a weakly
dominant strategy.

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:15.140 align:middle line:84%
So normally, with
auction equilibria

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:18.220 align:middle line:84%
in, say, first-price auctions,
the best way for you to bid

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:20.540 align:middle line:84%
depends on the way
your opponent bids.

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:23.540 align:middle line:84%
In this case, bidding
truthfully is best no matter

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:25.660 align:middle line:84%
how your opponent bids,
no matter what strategy

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:26.600 align:middle line:90%
your opponent uses.

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:27.100 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:28.340 align:middle line:90%
Question?

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:30.345 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I may have
missed it, but--

00:10:30.345 --> 00:10:31.220 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah, fine.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:34.380 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --why isn't
bidding b1 prime

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:38.420 align:middle line:84%
when your opponent bids b2 all
the way at the bottom better

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:39.360 align:middle line:90%
than v1?

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:40.400 align:middle line:90%
Like don't you pay--

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:40.780 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Let's see.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:42.020 align:middle line:90%
So there's six cases here.

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:45.000 align:middle line:90%
So you're in this one, case 1?

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.100 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's the
graph on the right.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:48.808 align:middle line:90%
And so you see--

00:10:48.808 --> 00:10:49.600 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: And here?

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:49.960 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:50.280 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: OK.

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:50.780 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:51.580 align:middle line:90%
Let's go over this.

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:55.800 align:middle line:84%
So let's suppose my
opponent bids b2 here.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:57.253 align:middle line:90%
What happens if I bid v1?

00:10:57.253 --> 00:10:58.920 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, because
it's second price.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:10:59.980 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Because the
second-price auction.

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:02.147 align:middle line:84%
It wouldn't be true if it
was a first-price auction.

00:11:02.147 --> 00:11:02.920 align:middle line:90%
Either way, I win.

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:05.040 align:middle line:84%
And the crucial point about
a second-price auction

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:08.518 align:middle line:84%
is when I win, the amount I pay
doesn't depend on what I bid.

00:11:08.518 --> 00:11:10.560 align:middle line:84%
So the crucial property
of a second-price auction

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:13.720 align:middle line:84%
is my bid can affect
whether I win.

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:17.020 align:middle line:84%
But if I win, the amount I
pay does not depend on my bid.

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:19.360 align:middle line:84%
It only depends on
my opponent's bid.

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:23.000 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, if I bid here
or here, I win either way,

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:24.360 align:middle line:90%
and I pay b2.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:25.240 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:27.640 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:30.523 align:middle line:84%
So now we've analyzed
this example.

00:11:30.523 --> 00:11:32.440 align:middle line:84%
I think you can see that
this kind of argument

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:33.607 align:middle line:90%
is actually going to extend.

00:11:33.607 --> 00:11:35.780 align:middle line:84%
We didn't really use the
uniform distribution here.

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:37.520 align:middle line:84%
This is a much more
general property

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:40.480 align:middle line:84%
of second-price auctions that
it's optimal to bid truthfully.

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:45.900 align:middle line:84%
But I want to make one kind of
interesting observation here.

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:47.900 align:middle line:84%
We've looked at two
different auction formats,

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:50.025 align:middle line:84%
and we've solved for an
equilibrium in each auction

00:11:50.025 --> 00:11:50.620 align:middle line:90%
format.

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:53.460 align:middle line:84%
So a natural question to
ask is, which auction format

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:54.880 align:middle line:90%
is better for the auctioneer?

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:58.220 align:middle line:90%


00:11:58.220 --> 00:12:01.600 align:middle line:84%
Which auction does
the auctioneer prefer?

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:07.280 align:middle line:90%


00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:09.780 align:middle line:84%
Well, to say "prefer," we have
to talk about the auctioneers

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:10.680 align:middle line:90%
preferences.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:12.780 align:middle line:84%
Let's assume that the
auctioneer likes money

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:14.060 align:middle line:90%
and they're risk neutral.

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:16.460 align:middle line:84%
So the goal of the
auctioneer is to maximize

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:18.580 align:middle line:84%
their expected revenue
in the auction.

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:22.620 align:middle line:84%
So now let's compute
their expected revenue

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:24.440 align:middle line:90%
in the first-price auction.

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:34.500 align:middle line:90%


00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:37.570 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's first just
look at their revenue.

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:38.690 align:middle line:90%
Maybe we'll call it pi.

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:40.270 align:middle line:84%
Pi is a good
notation for revenue.

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:45.270 align:middle line:84%
So what is their revenue
in the first-price auction

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:48.590 align:middle line:84%
if bidder 1's value
for the good is v1,

00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:51.030 align:middle line:84%
and bidder two's value
for the good is v2,

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:54.190 align:middle line:84%
and the bidders are following
the first-price auction

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:56.990 align:middle line:90%
strategy.

00:12:56.990 --> 00:13:01.790 align:middle line:84%
Well, that means bidder 1
is going to bid v1 over 2.

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:04.545 align:middle line:84%
Bidder 2 is going
to bid v2 over 2.

00:13:04.545 --> 00:13:07.170 align:middle line:84%
And how much revenue then am I
going to make as the auctioneer?

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:12.730 align:middle line:90%


00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:13.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:14.670 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Whichever
of those is larger.

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:15.130 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:16.838 align:middle line:84%
So one way of writing
that mathematically

00:13:16.838 --> 00:13:18.670 align:middle line:84%
is just the maximum
of these two.

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:22.970 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to make the maximum
of v1 over 2 and v2 over 2.

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:23.630 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:26.990 align:middle line:84%
Well, this is how
much player 1 bids.

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:28.910 align:middle line:90%
This is how much player 2 bids.

00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:30.630 align:middle line:90%
In a first-price auction.

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:34.370 align:middle line:84%
I collect the higher bid because
the higher bidder wins the item

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:35.530 align:middle line:90%
and pays their bid.

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:37.570 align:middle line:84%
So if v1 over 2
is bigger, they're

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:41.210 align:middle line:84%
going to-- player 1 wins the
auction and pays v1 over 2.

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:43.210 align:middle line:90%
If bidder 2's bid is higher--

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:46.630 align:middle line:84%
that is if v2 over 2 is higher,
then bidder 2 wins the auction.

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:50.970 align:middle line:84%
They pay their bid v2 over
2, and this is what I get.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:52.610 align:middle line:84%
So this is telling
me what my revenue

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:56.530 align:middle line:84%
is for a given realization
of the valuations of the two

00:13:56.530 --> 00:13:57.230 align:middle line:90%
bidders.

00:13:57.230 --> 00:13:59.490 align:middle line:84%
But now I want to take
expectations here.

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:08.690 align:middle line:84%
So my expected revenue is equal
to the expectation of this.

00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:10.750 align:middle line:84%
Maybe as a trick, I'll
just pull out the half.

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:14.116 align:middle line:84%
I can always-- the max of
v1 over 2 and v2 over 2

00:14:14.116 --> 00:14:17.070 align:middle line:84%
is just half of the
maximum of v1 and v2.

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:19.730 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll write
this as one half

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:23.790 align:middle line:84%
of the expectation
of the max of v1, v2.

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:29.030 align:middle line:90%


00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:32.750 align:middle line:84%
And this is an
expression that comes up

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:34.730 align:middle line:84%
a lot for uniform
random variables.

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:37.810 align:middle line:84%
It turns out the answer to
this expectation is 2/3.

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:40.310 align:middle line:84%
But I want to give a little
geometric argument for that.

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:41.530 align:middle line:90%
But let's just fill it in.

00:14:41.530 --> 00:14:43.990 align:middle line:84%
If this is 2/3, and I
multiply it by a half,

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:48.470 align:middle line:84%
then what I'm going
to get is one third.

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:51.630 align:middle line:84%
Where did this 2/3
magically come from?

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:52.370 align:middle line:90%
Well let's think.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:54.230 align:middle line:84%
Well, first it's the
number makes sense.

00:14:54.230 --> 00:14:57.530 align:middle line:84%
I mean, v1 and v2 are both
uniformly distributed on 0, 1.

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:00.090 align:middle line:84%
So this number should
be between 0 and 1.

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:01.190 align:middle line:90%
That makes sense.

00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:05.330 align:middle line:84%
Now, the expectation of
v1 by itself is a half.

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:07.070 align:middle line:84%
If you're uniformly
distributed on 0, 1,

00:15:07.070 --> 00:15:08.470 align:middle line:90%
the expectation is a half.

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:10.590 align:middle line:84%
So certainly, it makes
sense that the expectation

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:12.230 align:middle line:84%
of the maximum of
these two numbers

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:14.810 align:middle line:84%
should be higher than the
expectation of just one of them.

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:17.570 align:middle line:84%
So we'd expect to get a
number between one half and 1.

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:18.970 align:middle line:90%
So 2/3 makes sense.

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:20.748 align:middle line:90%
Why do we get exactly 2/3?

00:15:20.748 --> 00:15:22.790 align:middle line:84%
There's actually a really
nice geometric argument

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:23.790 align:middle line:90%
that I want to show you.

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:28.170 align:middle line:84%
So we could think of v1 and
v2 as living on this line.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:31.490 align:middle line:84%
Maybe here is v2,
maybe here is v1.

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:34.010 align:middle line:84%
And these are
uniformly distributed.

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:38.610 align:middle line:84%
The trick is actually to connect
this line to make it a circle.

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:42.210 align:middle line:84%
So now let's view
this as a circle,

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:46.590 align:middle line:90%
where this point is 0 and 1.

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:49.770 align:middle line:84%
So I've basically taken this,
and I've just bent it around

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:52.130 align:middle line:90%
to look like this.

00:15:52.130 --> 00:16:00.290 align:middle line:90%
So now I have v2 and v1 here.

00:16:00.290 --> 00:16:15.050 align:middle line:84%
Well, now, the maximum of v1 and
v2 is going to be this length.

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:17.570 align:middle line:84%
But now, if I think
about the circle,

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:19.630 align:middle line:84%
because things are
uniformly distributed,

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:21.970 align:middle line:84%
I basically just have three
uniformly distributed points

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:22.870 align:middle line:90%
on the circle.

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:24.953 align:middle line:84%
I mean, this one
is a fixed point.

00:16:24.953 --> 00:16:26.870 align:middle line:84%
It's not uniform, but
it's as if it's uniform.

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:28.120 align:middle line:90%
It wouldn't make a difference.

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:30.390 align:middle line:84%
So if I have three
points on a circle,

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:33.188 align:middle line:84%
the expected distance
between any consecutive ones

00:16:33.188 --> 00:16:34.230 align:middle line:90%
is going to be one third.

00:16:34.230 --> 00:16:36.790 align:middle line:84%
In expectation, this is
a third, this is a third,

00:16:36.790 --> 00:16:37.950 align:middle line:90%
and this is a third.

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:41.102 align:middle line:84%
So the expectation of two of
the segments is going to be 2/3.

00:16:41.102 --> 00:16:42.810 align:middle line:84%
It's maybe not quite
a rigorous argument,

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:45.670 align:middle line:84%
but I think that should give
you intuition for why this

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:49.510 align:middle line:84%
expectation is 2/3, because
you just effectively have three

00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:51.070 align:middle line:90%
points on the circle.

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:53.790 align:middle line:90%


00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:56.230 align:middle line:84%
So now we see that in
the first-price auction,

00:16:56.230 --> 00:16:58.670 align:middle line:84%
the designer's expected
revenue is one third.

00:16:58.670 --> 00:17:01.950 align:middle line:84%
Let's now look at the
second-price auction.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:09.990 align:middle line:84%
So in the second-price auction,
if bidder 1's valuation is v1,

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:15.150 align:middle line:84%
bidder 2's valuation
is v2, and both bidders

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:18.390 align:middle line:84%
follow their equilibrium
strategy of bidding truthfully,

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:20.510 align:middle line:84%
then what is the
auctioneer's revenue going

00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:22.230 align:middle line:90%
to be in this case?

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:22.730 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:23.992 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The minimum of v1.

00:17:23.992 --> 00:17:25.450 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: The
minimum of v1 and v2.

00:17:25.450 --> 00:17:29.662 align:middle line:90%


00:17:29.662 --> 00:17:32.920 align:middle line:84%
Because in this case,
bidder 1 bids v1.

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:35.080 align:middle line:90%
Bidder 2 bids v2.

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:37.080 align:middle line:84%
The higher bidder wins
the item, but they

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:38.220 align:middle line:90%
don't pay their own bid.

00:17:38.220 --> 00:17:40.040 align:middle line:84%
They pay the
second-highest bid, which

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:42.100 align:middle line:84%
in this case, what's
the second-highest bid?

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:43.620 align:middle line:84%
It's the minimum
of these two bids.

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:45.520 align:middle line:84%
If there's only two
bids, the second-highest

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:47.000 align:middle line:90%
is the minimum of the two.

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:49.640 align:middle line:84%
So here, we nicely
see the trade-off.

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:53.600 align:middle line:84%
In a first-price auction, you
get the higher of the two bids,

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:57.505 align:middle line:84%
but each of the bids is smaller,
because the bidders anticipate

00:17:57.505 --> 00:17:59.880 align:middle line:84%
that it's a first-price auction,
so they shade their bids

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:01.240 align:middle line:90%
downward.

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:04.460 align:middle line:84%
In a second-price auction, you
get the lower of the two bids.

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:06.140 align:middle line:90%
That hurts the auctioneer.

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:08.640 align:middle line:84%
But the benefit is people are
bidding higher because they're

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:10.100 align:middle line:90%
bidding their true valuations.

00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:13.840 align:middle line:84%
So the question is, which
of these two forces wins?

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:16.340 align:middle line:90%
Well let's compute this.

00:18:16.340 --> 00:18:19.720 align:middle line:90%
We get the expectation of pi--

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:20.840 align:middle line:90%
big pi, I guess--

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:28.300 align:middle line:84%
of v1, v2 equals, well, just
the expectation of the min

00:18:28.300 --> 00:18:30.180 align:middle line:90%
of v1, v2.

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:32.960 align:middle line:84%
And if we use our
little graph over there,

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:36.390 align:middle line:84%
can we guess what
this expectation is?

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:36.890 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:18:36.890 --> 00:18:37.723 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: One third.

00:18:37.723 --> 00:18:39.460 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: One
third, because now I

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:42.240 align:middle line:84%
have these three
points on the circle.

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:43.740 align:middle line:84%
The minimum of the
two is just going

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:48.480 align:middle line:84%
to be the length of the segment
between two consecutive points.

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:51.900 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be half as
big as the length between--

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:53.300 align:middle line:90%
over two segments.

00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:55.080 align:middle line:84%
So I'm exactly going
to get one third.

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:59.260 align:middle line:90%


00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:01.500 align:middle line:84%
So what you see here is
actually these two effects

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:03.580 align:middle line:90%
I described exactly offset.

00:19:03.580 --> 00:19:06.720 align:middle line:84%
In the first-price auction,
you get paid the highest bid,

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:08.580 align:middle line:90%
but people choose to bid less.

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:11.380 align:middle line:84%
In the second-price auction,
you get the smaller bid,

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:13.340 align:middle line:90%
but people choose to bid more.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:15.420 align:middle line:84%
And those two effects
exactly offset.

00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:17.020 align:middle line:84%
And the designer's
expected revenue

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:19.500 align:middle line:84%
is exactly one
third in each case.

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:21.100 align:middle line:84%
Now, this was a
very simple example

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:22.615 align:middle line:90%
with uniform distributions.

00:19:22.615 --> 00:19:24.740 align:middle line:84%
So you might think, oh,
this is just a coincidence.

00:19:24.740 --> 00:19:25.640 align:middle line:90%
This is a fluke.

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:28.120 align:middle line:84%
It turns out this is a very
deep and general phenomenon,

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:31.160 align:middle line:84%
that many different
auction formats will

00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:34.260 align:middle line:84%
generate the same expected
revenue for the auctioneer.

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:36.480 align:middle line:84%
And that's something that
we'll talk about a bit

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:38.120 align:middle line:90%
later in the course.

00:19:38.120 --> 00:19:42.540 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on this?

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:43.040 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:46.400 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The trick for finding
the maximum and minimum,

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:47.980 align:middle line:84%
is that applicable
if you had more?

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:48.320 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:48.820 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:50.280 align:middle line:90%
So maybe let's write this down.

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:56.140 align:middle line:84%
So if I did-- if everything
was uniform 0, 1 and I had n--

00:19:56.140 --> 00:20:05.040 align:middle line:84%
I had v1 through vn, then the
expectation of the max of v1

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:08.520 align:middle line:84%
through vn-- and this is
kind of a good fact to know--

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:11.500 align:middle line:84%
guess, can you see what--
if we complete the pattern,

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:13.380 align:middle line:90%
we got 2/3 when n was 2.

00:20:13.380 --> 00:20:16.180 align:middle line:84%
What do you think this
is going to be now?

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:17.500 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: n over n plus 1.

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:18.500 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:22.460 align:middle line:90%


00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:24.980 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, you can
actually go further.

00:20:24.980 --> 00:20:27.920 align:middle line:84%
If I wanted the expectation
of the second highest,

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:30.520 align:middle line:84%
it would be n minus
1 over n plus 1.

00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:33.140 align:middle line:84%
The third highest, n
minus 2 over n plus 1 all

00:20:33.140 --> 00:20:41.660 align:middle line:84%
the way down to the
expectation of the min

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:45.420 align:middle line:90%
equals 1 over n plus 1.

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:47.313 align:middle line:84%
So there's a very
nice structure.

00:20:47.313 --> 00:20:49.980 align:middle line:84%
I could look at the lowest, the
second lowest, the third lowest,

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:51.240 align:middle line:90%
all the way up to the highest.

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:54.100 align:middle line:84%
And those are exactly
going to be equally spaced.

00:20:54.100 --> 00:20:57.660 align:middle line:84%
And they're separated
exactly by 1 over n plus 1.

00:20:57.660 --> 00:21:00.190 align:middle line:84%
And you can use
calculus to verify this,

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:02.440 align:middle line:84%
but I think this is a nice
intuition for what goes on.

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:05.160 align:middle line:84%
And you could try to
formalize that intuition.

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:07.780 align:middle line:90%


00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:09.480 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:10.440 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:12.960 align:middle line:90%


00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:17.620 align:middle line:84%
So now, as I said, this
was a very simple example.

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:19.120 align:middle line:84%
You might worry
that our conclusions

00:21:19.120 --> 00:21:21.640 align:middle line:90%
are really sensitive to that.

00:21:21.640 --> 00:21:25.200 align:middle line:84%
What we want to do now is study
a more general auction setup,

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:27.830 align:middle line:84%
where instead of two
bidders, we have n bidders,

00:21:27.830 --> 00:21:30.080 align:middle line:84%
and instead of assuming their
valuations are uniformly

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:32.700 align:middle line:84%
distributed, we allow for
more general valuations.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:35.440 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to look
at a more general model.

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:37.320 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
look at, in general,

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:54.880 align:middle line:84%
what's maybe called the
symmetric independent private

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:55.580 align:middle line:90%
values model.

00:21:55.580 --> 00:22:00.560 align:middle line:84%
Let me explain what these
words mean as we go through.

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:02.460 align:middle line:84%
So now we're going
to have n bidders.

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:08.500 align:middle line:84%
So we have bidders
i equals 1 up to n.

00:22:08.500 --> 00:22:13.040 align:middle line:90%


00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:16.700 align:middle line:84%
And the bidders are
symmetric in the sense

00:22:16.700 --> 00:22:20.900 align:middle line:84%
that the distribution from which
each bidder's valuation is drawn

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:22.060 align:middle line:90%
is the same.

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:25.440 align:middle line:84%
So of course, when we
actually run the auction,

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:27.640 align:middle line:84%
someone might value the
good more than someone else.

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:29.375 align:middle line:84%
But before we start
the auction, we

00:22:29.375 --> 00:22:31.500 align:middle line:84%
don't believe that the
distribution of one player's

00:22:31.500 --> 00:22:34.060 align:middle line:84%
valuation is higher than the
distribution of another player's

00:22:34.060 --> 00:22:34.633 align:middle line:90%
valuation.

00:22:34.633 --> 00:22:36.800 align:middle line:84%
In reality, this is probably
not a great assumption,

00:22:36.800 --> 00:22:38.500 align:middle line:84%
but this simplifies
things, and we can talk

00:22:38.500 --> 00:22:39.600 align:middle line:90%
later about how it extends.

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:40.558 align:middle line:90%
So what does that mean?

00:22:40.558 --> 00:22:47.220 align:middle line:84%
It means that for each
bidder i the valuation vi--

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:49.720 align:middle line:84%
well, before we said the
valuation was in 0, 1.

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:51.160 align:middle line:90%
Let's be a little more general.

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:54.720 align:middle line:84%
Let's say this lives in the
interval between v lower bar

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:55.620 align:middle line:90%
and v upper bar.

00:22:55.620 --> 00:22:58.660 align:middle line:84%
So this is the lowest possible
valuation a bidder could have.

00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:00.540 align:middle line:84%
And this is the highest
possible valuation

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:02.020 align:middle line:90%
that a bidder could have.

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:03.820 align:middle line:84%
Notice I don't have
subscripts here

00:23:03.820 --> 00:23:07.120 align:middle line:84%
because the range of valuations
is the same for all the players.

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:10.420 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't depend on the
identity of the bidder.

00:23:10.420 --> 00:23:22.510 align:middle line:84%
And we'll say this is
distributed according to a CDF--

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:24.790 align:middle line:84%
that's a Cumulative
Distribution Function--

00:23:24.790 --> 00:23:29.670 align:middle line:84%
capital F and a PDF, a
Probability Density Function,

00:23:29.670 --> 00:23:30.170 align:middle line:90%
little f.

00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:33.350 align:middle line:84%
And I'll explain
what these mean.

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:36.170 align:middle line:84%
So this is just a way to
describe a distribution.

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:39.390 align:middle line:84%
If you've taken a probability
class, which is, I think,

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:40.970 align:middle line:84%
technically a
prerequisite for this,

00:23:40.970 --> 00:23:42.370 align:middle line:90%
you would have gone over this.

00:23:42.370 --> 00:23:43.770 align:middle line:90%
But what are these things?

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:51.030 align:middle line:84%
Well, F of v just tells
us the probability

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:54.190 align:middle line:84%
that the valuation
is below v. So

00:23:54.190 --> 00:23:59.390 align:middle line:84%
F of v is defined to be the
probability that, say, vi

00:23:59.390 --> 00:24:03.790 align:middle line:90%
is less than or equal to v.

00:24:03.790 --> 00:24:06.550 align:middle line:90%
So F is an increasing function.

00:24:06.550 --> 00:24:10.150 align:middle line:84%
If I plug In v lower
bar, I'm going to get 0,

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:14.450 align:middle line:84%
because the probability that
vi is below v lower bar is 0.

00:24:14.450 --> 00:24:18.410 align:middle line:84%
If I plug in v
upper bar, then I'm

00:24:18.410 --> 00:24:21.890 align:middle line:84%
going to get 1, because
the probability that vi

00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:24.250 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to v upper bar is 1.

00:24:24.250 --> 00:24:26.490 align:middle line:84%
And then it's going
to fill in between.

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:29.210 align:middle line:84%
When I say it has a PDF
f, What that means is I

00:24:29.210 --> 00:24:33.410 align:middle line:84%
can express this probability
as an integral from v lower bar

00:24:33.410 --> 00:24:37.410 align:middle line:90%
to v of f of x dx.

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:40.410 align:middle line:90%


00:24:40.410 --> 00:24:45.370 align:middle line:84%
So that means we can
visualize the distribution.

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:48.050 align:middle line:84%
Here's v lower bar,
here's v upper bar

00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:52.570 align:middle line:90%
by some density like this.

00:24:52.570 --> 00:24:54.970 align:middle line:84%
And what this
says, the peaks say

00:24:54.970 --> 00:24:58.885 align:middle line:84%
these are places where the
valuation is very likely to be.

00:24:58.885 --> 00:25:01.010 align:middle line:84%
And then the valleys are
places where the valuation

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:02.530 align:middle line:90%
is less likely to be.

00:25:02.530 --> 00:25:10.910 align:middle line:84%
And what we're saying
is if we fix v here,

00:25:10.910 --> 00:25:14.670 align:middle line:84%
the area under this curve
is exactly the probability

00:25:14.670 --> 00:25:17.630 align:middle line:84%
that the valuation lies
between v lower bar and v.

00:25:17.630 --> 00:25:21.138 align:middle line:84%
So what that means, one
implication of that,

00:25:21.138 --> 00:25:23.430 align:middle line:84%
is that it must be that the
area under the entire curve

00:25:23.430 --> 00:25:26.390 align:middle line:84%
is 1, because the probability
that v lives somewhere

00:25:26.390 --> 00:25:26.910 align:middle line:90%
is just 1.

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:28.410 align:middle line:84%
That's just a fact
of probabilities.

00:25:28.410 --> 00:25:30.030 align:middle line:84%
So this is probably
a little quick

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:31.388 align:middle line:90%
if you haven't seen this before.

00:25:31.388 --> 00:25:33.930 align:middle line:84%
That is a prereq for the course,
and I'm happy to chat after,

00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:35.910 align:middle line:84%
or maybe you can
self-study if you

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:38.150 align:middle line:84%
haven't seen probability
distributions

00:25:38.150 --> 00:25:41.390 align:middle line:90%
or densities before.

00:25:41.390 --> 00:25:44.950 align:middle line:84%
So symmetry refers to
the fact that we're

00:25:44.950 --> 00:25:47.790 align:middle line:84%
using the same
distribution F and little

00:25:47.790 --> 00:25:49.290 align:middle line:90%
f for all the bidders.

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:52.550 align:middle line:84%
It's not varying
across the bidders.

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:57.990 align:middle line:84%
Independence means
that v1 through vn

00:25:57.990 --> 00:25:59.770 align:middle line:90%
are statistically independent.

00:25:59.770 --> 00:26:09.065 align:middle line:90%


00:26:09.065 --> 00:26:11.190 align:middle line:84%
This is the same assumption
we made in the example.

00:26:11.190 --> 00:26:13.690 align:middle line:84%
It's that if I know
my valuation v1,

00:26:13.690 --> 00:26:16.650 align:middle line:84%
that doesn't allow me to update
my beliefs about other players'

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:17.430 align:middle line:90%
valuations.

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:19.850 align:middle line:84%
So what it rules out is things
like positive or negative

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:22.810 align:middle line:84%
correlation, where when one
bidder's valuation is high,

00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:25.010 align:middle line:84%
everyone else's valuation
tends to be high.

00:26:25.010 --> 00:26:27.010 align:middle line:84%
Or when one bidder's
valuation is low,

00:26:27.010 --> 00:26:29.690 align:middle line:84%
everyone else's valuation
tends to be low.

00:26:29.690 --> 00:26:32.270 align:middle line:84%
This is also maybe
a strong assumption,

00:26:32.270 --> 00:26:34.050 align:middle line:84%
and we can, again,
maybe talk later

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:35.310 align:middle line:90%
about how it could be relaxed.

00:26:35.310 --> 00:26:38.670 align:middle line:84%
But this is what we're
going to assume for today.

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:42.600 align:middle line:90%


00:26:42.600 --> 00:26:47.750 align:middle line:84%
And I should maybe say one
thing about these endpoints.

00:26:47.750 --> 00:26:54.010 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to assume that 0 is
less than or equal to v bar,

00:26:54.010 --> 00:26:55.470 align:middle line:90%
strictly less than v upper bar.

00:26:55.470 --> 00:26:58.082 align:middle line:84%
So the agents always assign a
nonnegative value to the good.

00:26:58.082 --> 00:26:59.790 align:middle line:84%
I think this is a
pretty safe assumption.

00:26:59.790 --> 00:27:01.850 align:middle line:84%
If they really hated the good,
they could just throw it away.

00:27:01.850 --> 00:27:04.343 align:middle line:84%
So we're always assuming that
they value it at least 0.

00:27:04.343 --> 00:27:06.010 align:middle line:84%
They could value it
at 0, but they never

00:27:06.010 --> 00:27:07.530 align:middle line:90%
value it at a negative number.

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:13.870 align:middle line:90%


00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:15.990 align:middle line:90%
So we've talked about symmetry.

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:18.710 align:middle line:90%
We've talked about independence.

00:27:18.710 --> 00:27:20.670 align:middle line:84%
What private values
refers to-- and maybe

00:27:20.670 --> 00:27:21.730 align:middle line:90%
I'll give us the acronym.

00:27:21.730 --> 00:27:25.150 align:middle line:90%
This is SIPV.

00:27:25.150 --> 00:27:26.870 align:middle line:84%
Private values
refers to the fact

00:27:26.870 --> 00:27:31.550 align:middle line:84%
that each bidder knows exactly
how much he values the good.

00:27:31.550 --> 00:27:35.270 align:middle line:84%
And bidder i's
valuation doesn't depend

00:27:35.270 --> 00:27:37.430 align:middle line:90%
on other players' valuations.

00:27:37.430 --> 00:27:41.430 align:middle line:84%
So you might imagine, if we were
in an auction for a painting,

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:44.110 align:middle line:84%
and there was a chance that
I could resell the painting,

00:27:44.110 --> 00:27:46.230 align:middle line:84%
I might care about how
much other people value

00:27:46.230 --> 00:27:48.630 align:middle line:84%
the painting, because if
they value the painting more,

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:50.630 align:middle line:84%
I might think, wow, I
could resell it to them

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:52.750 align:middle line:84%
after the auction, and
therefore, my valuation

00:27:52.750 --> 00:27:54.090 align:middle line:90%
for the painting goes up.

00:27:54.090 --> 00:27:56.890 align:middle line:84%
So we're ruling that out
by assuming private values.

00:27:56.890 --> 00:27:59.510 align:middle line:84%
We're assuming that I know
my valuation for the good,

00:27:59.510 --> 00:28:03.012 align:middle line:84%
and how much I'm willing to
pay depends only on my signal

00:28:03.012 --> 00:28:04.720 align:middle line:84%
about, say, how much
I like the painting,

00:28:04.720 --> 00:28:10.280 align:middle line:84%
and not on how much anyone
else likes the painting.

00:28:10.280 --> 00:28:13.160 align:middle line:84%
So here we are, and we're
going to try to analyze

00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:15.840 align:middle line:90%
the first-price auction.

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:18.640 align:middle line:84%
We're going to try to
solve for an equilibrium

00:28:18.640 --> 00:28:20.340 align:middle line:90%
of the first-price auction.

00:28:20.340 --> 00:28:30.360 align:middle line:84%
So goal, solve for BNE, a
Bayes' Nash equilibrium,

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:34.280 align:middle line:84%
of the first-price auction
in the SIPV setting.

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:36.840 align:middle line:90%


00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:38.680 align:middle line:90%
I'll use a lot of acronyms--

00:28:38.680 --> 00:28:48.778 align:middle line:84%
of FPA, the First-Price
Auction in the SIPV setting.

00:28:48.778 --> 00:28:50.820 align:middle line:84%
Notice the setting and
the auction are different.

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:52.680 align:middle line:84%
The setting is
about who's involved

00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:54.600 align:middle line:84%
and how much they
value the good.

00:28:54.600 --> 00:28:56.825 align:middle line:84%
The auction format,
these are the rules

00:28:56.825 --> 00:28:58.200 align:middle line:84%
that the designer
gets to choose,

00:28:58.200 --> 00:29:00.140 align:middle line:84%
or the auctioneer
gets to choose.

00:29:00.140 --> 00:29:02.820 align:middle line:84%
The auctioneer can't choose
how much people value the good.

00:29:02.820 --> 00:29:04.200 align:middle line:84%
This is just a
fact of the world.

00:29:04.200 --> 00:29:05.540 align:middle line:90%
This is a fact of the setting.

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:07.580 align:middle line:84%
But the auction format
is something that

00:29:07.580 --> 00:29:10.380 align:middle line:90%
is chosen by the auctioneer.

00:29:10.380 --> 00:29:13.020 align:middle line:84%
Well, first, as always, we
have to figure out, well,

00:29:13.020 --> 00:29:15.980 align:middle line:84%
what is a strategy
for each player?

00:29:15.980 --> 00:29:18.600 align:middle line:84%
So what is a strategy
for player i?

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:22.580 align:middle line:90%


00:29:22.580 --> 00:29:24.940 align:middle line:84%
Well, a strategy needs
to say, how much does

00:29:24.940 --> 00:29:30.380 align:middle line:84%
player i bid as a function of
their valuation for the good.

00:29:30.380 --> 00:29:37.000 align:middle line:84%
So a strategy for player i is
going to be a function bi from--

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:42.060 align:middle line:90%


00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:45.940 align:middle line:90%
maybe I'll write this.

00:29:45.940 --> 00:29:48.100 align:middle line:90%
So what does this mean?

00:29:48.100 --> 00:29:51.540 align:middle line:84%
It means that for each
valuation of the item that I

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:55.080 align:middle line:84%
could actually have, bi
is my bidding function.

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:57.640 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes that's the
terminology we'll use.

00:29:57.640 --> 00:30:02.560 align:middle line:90%


00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:06.080 align:middle line:84%
My bidding function
says, for each valuation,

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:09.560 align:middle line:84%
say, vi that I could
have, my bidding function

00:30:09.560 --> 00:30:13.760 align:middle line:84%
spits out bi of vi, which
is the bid that I will

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:16.680 align:middle line:90%
make if my valuation is vi.

00:30:16.680 --> 00:30:18.620 align:middle line:84%
And just using the
rules of the auction,

00:30:18.620 --> 00:30:21.300 align:middle line:84%
we're assuming that bids can
be any nonnegative number.

00:30:21.300 --> 00:30:23.318 align:middle line:90%
I can't bid a negative amount.

00:30:23.318 --> 00:30:24.860 align:middle line:84%
I can't ask the
auctioneer to pay me.

00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:26.360 align:middle line:90%
That would be a bit strange.

00:30:26.360 --> 00:30:28.980 align:middle line:84%
But I can bid any nonnegative
number that I want.

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:32.680 align:middle line:90%


00:30:32.680 --> 00:30:37.240 align:middle line:84%
And an equilibrium is going
to specify a strategy profile.

00:30:37.240 --> 00:30:39.440 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to specify
a bidding function

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:42.000 align:middle line:90%
for each of the n bidders.

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:46.320 align:middle line:84%
So as before, this is kind
of a difficult problem

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:48.920 align:middle line:84%
because we're trying to
solve for functions, not just

00:30:48.920 --> 00:30:49.778 align:middle line:90%
single numbers.

00:30:49.778 --> 00:30:50.820 align:middle line:90%
And these are really big.

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:51.700 align:middle line:90%
There's a lot of--

00:30:51.700 --> 00:30:53.700 align:middle line:84%
each player is choosing
infinitely many numbers.

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:55.117 align:middle line:84%
How they bid if
their valuation is

00:30:55.117 --> 00:30:58.380 align:middle line:84%
1, how they'd bid if the
valuation is 2, 2.3, 2.5,

00:30:58.380 --> 00:31:01.220 align:middle line:84%
there's too many
things to solve for.

00:31:01.220 --> 00:31:04.760 align:middle line:84%
Before, recall that we got
around this by guessing.

00:31:04.760 --> 00:31:09.220 align:middle line:84%
We guessed that there would
be an equilibrium where

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:12.140 align:middle line:84%
each bidding function was
just a linear function.

00:31:12.140 --> 00:31:13.860 align:middle line:84%
And that simplified
things a lot.

00:31:13.860 --> 00:31:16.980 align:middle line:84%
It reduced our problem to just
solving for a single parameter.

00:31:16.980 --> 00:31:20.220 align:middle line:84%
But that only worked in the
special uniform example.

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:22.180 align:middle line:84%
So beyond the
uniform distribution,

00:31:22.180 --> 00:31:23.860 align:middle line:84%
there's no reason
to think that people

00:31:23.860 --> 00:31:25.700 align:middle line:84%
are going to use a
linear bidding function

00:31:25.700 --> 00:31:28.468 align:middle line:84%
and bid exactly one
third of their valuation,

00:31:28.468 --> 00:31:29.760 align:middle line:90%
or one half of their valuation.

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:31.523 align:middle line:84%
So we have to be a
bit more general.

00:31:31.523 --> 00:31:32.940 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:31:32.940 --> 00:31:34.420 align:middle line:90%
going to also make a guess.

00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:39.940 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
guess, or look for,

00:31:39.940 --> 00:32:00.400 align:middle line:84%
a symmetric increasing
differentiable equilibrium.

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:02.480 align:middle line:84%
Let's go through what
all these words mean.

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:05.720 align:middle line:84%
Well, we're going to
look for an equilibrium

00:32:05.720 --> 00:32:09.240 align:middle line:84%
where every bidder uses
the bidding function

00:32:09.240 --> 00:32:12.140 align:middle line:90%
beta from v bar.

00:32:12.140 --> 00:32:26.880 align:middle line:90%


00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:30.800 align:middle line:84%
so we're going to say, look,
because the bidders are

00:32:30.800 --> 00:32:33.720 align:middle line:84%
x antisymmetric,
their valuations

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:35.680 align:middle line:84%
are drawn from the
same distribution,

00:32:35.680 --> 00:32:37.280 align:middle line:84%
we're going to look
for an equilibrium

00:32:37.280 --> 00:32:39.240 align:middle line:84%
where all the bidders
use the same bidding

00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:41.000 align:middle line:90%
function, the same strategy.

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:44.400 align:middle line:84%
So bidder 1, bidder 2, all
the way up to bidder n,

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:45.362 align:middle line:90%
use this function beta.

00:32:45.362 --> 00:32:47.320 align:middle line:84%
So if you wanted to be
formal what we're saying

00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:52.980 align:middle line:84%
is b1 equals b2
equals bn equals beta.

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:56.610 align:middle line:90%


00:32:56.610 --> 00:32:59.650 align:middle line:84%
So everyone uses the
same bidding function.

00:32:59.650 --> 00:33:02.370 align:middle line:84%
And we're assuming that this
bidding function is increasing.

00:33:02.370 --> 00:33:03.530 align:middle line:90%
That makes sense.

00:33:03.530 --> 00:33:05.730 align:middle line:84%
If you value the good
more, it seems intuitive

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:09.450 align:middle line:84%
that you should bid more for
the item because you care more--

00:33:09.450 --> 00:33:12.430 align:middle line:84%
you assign more utility
to winning the items.

00:33:12.430 --> 00:33:15.850 align:middle line:84%
You want to increase your
probability of winning.

00:33:15.850 --> 00:33:18.170 align:middle line:84%
And this is just kind of
a technical mathematical

00:33:18.170 --> 00:33:19.050 align:middle line:90%
assumption.

00:33:19.050 --> 00:33:20.910 align:middle line:84%
We want to be able
to apply calculus.

00:33:20.910 --> 00:33:23.470 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to assume
that beta is differentiable.

00:33:23.470 --> 00:33:27.073 align:middle line:84%
That is, we can always take the
derivative of beta, beta prime,

00:33:27.073 --> 00:33:28.490 align:middle line:84%
and that that
derivative is always

00:33:28.490 --> 00:33:30.210 align:middle line:90%
going to be strictly positive.

00:33:30.210 --> 00:33:32.910 align:middle line:84%
That's basically increasing, a
little stronger than increasing.

00:33:32.910 --> 00:33:35.530 align:middle line:90%


00:33:35.530 --> 00:33:38.130 align:middle line:84%
And now we've kind of
reduced our problem.

00:33:38.130 --> 00:33:41.510 align:middle line:84%
Instead of looking for n
arbitrary bidding functions,

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:43.760 align:middle line:84%
we're now looking for a
single bidding function beta.

00:33:43.760 --> 00:33:46.010 align:middle line:84%
And we want to see if we can
find an equilibrium where

00:33:46.010 --> 00:33:50.050 align:middle line:84%
every single bidder uses
this bidding function beta.

00:33:50.050 --> 00:33:51.613 align:middle line:84%
Any questions here
on our approach?

00:33:51.613 --> 00:33:53.530 align:middle line:84%
Because we're going to
get into a little math,

00:33:53.530 --> 00:33:57.610 align:middle line:84%
so I want to make sure
the approach is clear.

00:33:57.610 --> 00:34:00.390 align:middle line:90%


00:34:00.390 --> 00:34:03.450 align:middle line:90%
So what do we need to do?

00:34:03.450 --> 00:34:05.550 align:middle line:84%
How are we going
to solve for beta?

00:34:05.550 --> 00:34:08.070 align:middle line:84%
Well, if this is going
to be an equilibrium,

00:34:08.070 --> 00:34:11.230 align:middle line:84%
it better be that if everyone
else is falling the bidding

00:34:11.230 --> 00:34:13.949 align:middle line:84%
strategy beta, the
bidding function beta,

00:34:13.949 --> 00:34:17.570 align:middle line:84%
it's optimal for me to also
choose the bidding strategy

00:34:17.570 --> 00:34:18.630 align:middle line:90%
beta.

00:34:18.630 --> 00:34:21.550 align:middle line:84%
So what we want to say
is let's consider--

00:34:21.550 --> 00:34:22.635 align:middle line:90%
let's focus on bidder 1.

00:34:22.635 --> 00:34:24.010 align:middle line:84%
It just makes it
a little easier.

00:34:24.010 --> 00:34:28.389 align:middle line:90%


00:34:28.389 --> 00:34:31.110 align:middle line:84%
So put yourself in
the shoes of bidder 1.

00:34:31.110 --> 00:34:35.670 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that-- I'm going to
write-- define a function here

00:34:35.670 --> 00:34:43.790 align:middle line:90%
U1 of b1 given v1 beta.

00:34:43.790 --> 00:34:45.710 align:middle line:84%
Let me describe in
words what this means.

00:34:45.710 --> 00:34:47.630 align:middle line:90%
I want to say I'm bidder 1.

00:34:47.630 --> 00:34:48.949 align:middle line:90%
What do I know?

00:34:48.949 --> 00:34:51.909 align:middle line:84%
Well, I know my true
valuation for the good is v1.

00:34:51.909 --> 00:34:53.190 align:middle line:90%
So this is my valuation.

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:57.290 align:middle line:90%


00:34:57.290 --> 00:35:00.370 align:middle line:84%
And let me suppose that
all of my opponents

00:35:00.370 --> 00:35:02.810 align:middle line:84%
are following the
bidding strategy beta.

00:35:02.810 --> 00:35:10.130 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm supposing
here is that b2 equals

00:35:10.130 --> 00:35:14.430 align:middle line:90%
b3 equals bn equals beta.

00:35:14.430 --> 00:35:17.930 align:middle line:84%
So I assume that player 2 is
using the bidding function beta.

00:35:17.930 --> 00:35:19.970 align:middle line:84%
Player 3 is using the
bidding function beta,

00:35:19.970 --> 00:35:22.512 align:middle line:84%
all the way up to player n is
also using the bidding function

00:35:22.512 --> 00:35:23.370 align:middle line:90%
beta.

00:35:23.370 --> 00:35:25.370 align:middle line:84%
And I want to say,
OK, here I am.

00:35:25.370 --> 00:35:29.010 align:middle line:84%
I believe that my opponent is
using these bidding functions.

00:35:29.010 --> 00:35:32.050 align:middle line:84%
I know my true valuation
v1 for the good.

00:35:32.050 --> 00:35:35.030 align:middle line:84%
What I want to compute
is what is my utility

00:35:35.030 --> 00:35:36.770 align:middle line:90%
if I were to bid b1.

00:35:36.770 --> 00:35:38.230 align:middle line:90%
So b1 is just a number.

00:35:38.230 --> 00:35:42.410 align:middle line:90%


00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:46.210 align:middle line:84%
This is not a function,
it's just a number.

00:35:46.210 --> 00:35:47.887 align:middle line:84%
And once I compute
this function,

00:35:47.887 --> 00:35:49.470 align:middle line:84%
then I can start
thinking about-- then

00:35:49.470 --> 00:35:53.270 align:middle line:84%
I can maximize this function
and find the optimal bid for me.

00:35:53.270 --> 00:35:55.310 align:middle line:84%
But I need to put
in this information

00:35:55.310 --> 00:35:57.790 align:middle line:84%
because the best way for
me to bid in this auction

00:35:57.790 --> 00:35:59.930 align:middle line:84%
is going to depend on how
much I value the good,

00:35:59.930 --> 00:36:01.630 align:middle line:84%
and it's also going
to depend on what I

00:36:01.630 --> 00:36:02.970 align:middle line:90%
believe my opponents are using.

00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:06.630 align:middle line:84%
So that's why I need
all this notation here.

00:36:06.630 --> 00:36:08.990 align:middle line:90%
And this is the key quantity.

00:36:08.990 --> 00:36:15.630 align:middle line:90%
So let's try to--

00:36:15.630 --> 00:36:16.290 align:middle line:90%
any guesses?

00:36:16.290 --> 00:36:22.202 align:middle line:84%
Can we write out what this
expression is going to be?

00:36:22.202 --> 00:36:23.410 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's think it through.

00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:28.250 align:middle line:84%
If I lose the auction, my
payoff is 0, because if I lose,

00:36:28.250 --> 00:36:30.950 align:middle line:84%
I don't get the good and
I don't pay anything.

00:36:30.950 --> 00:36:32.450 align:middle line:84%
So we just have to
figure out, well,

00:36:32.450 --> 00:36:33.930 align:middle line:90%
what is my utility going to be?

00:36:33.930 --> 00:36:35.270 align:middle line:90%
Let's suppose I win.

00:36:35.270 --> 00:36:38.070 align:middle line:84%
If I win, and my
valuation is v1,

00:36:38.070 --> 00:36:43.630 align:middle line:84%
and my bid is V1, then what
will my utility be if I win?

00:36:43.630 --> 00:36:44.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:36:44.130 --> 00:36:45.328 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: V1 minus b1.

00:36:45.328 --> 00:36:46.370 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly, right.

00:36:46.370 --> 00:36:49.890 align:middle line:90%


00:36:49.890 --> 00:36:52.250 align:middle line:90%
So this is my utility if I win.

00:36:52.250 --> 00:36:54.770 align:middle line:84%
But I only get that
utility if I do win.

00:36:54.770 --> 00:36:56.970 align:middle line:84%
So now I have to multiply
that by the probability

00:36:56.970 --> 00:36:58.130 align:middle line:90%
that I actually win.

00:36:58.130 --> 00:37:00.690 align:middle line:84%
What is the
probability that I win?

00:37:00.690 --> 00:37:03.057 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's the
probability that-- well,

00:37:03.057 --> 00:37:04.390 align:middle line:90%
what does it mean for me to win?

00:37:04.390 --> 00:37:08.690 align:middle line:84%
I win if my bid is higher
than everyone else's bid.

00:37:08.690 --> 00:37:10.930 align:middle line:84%
Now, there's always
this question of ties.

00:37:10.930 --> 00:37:15.290 align:middle line:84%
Because this bidding function
is strictly increasing,

00:37:15.290 --> 00:37:18.158 align:middle line:84%
any single bid by my opponents
is going to have probability 0.

00:37:18.158 --> 00:37:19.950 align:middle line:84%
So I don't really have
to worry about ties.

00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:22.130 align:middle line:84%
So let me just put
in the probability

00:37:22.130 --> 00:37:25.770 align:middle line:84%
maybe that my bid is strictly
higher than all my opponents.

00:37:25.770 --> 00:37:30.650 align:middle line:84%
So that's the probability that,
b1 is greater than-- well, it

00:37:30.650 --> 00:37:33.610 align:middle line:84%
has to be greater than
how much b2 bids, b3 bids,

00:37:33.610 --> 00:37:35.107 align:middle line:84%
b4-- well that's
just the maximum.

00:37:35.107 --> 00:37:37.190 align:middle line:84%
Let me just take the maximum
of all of their bids.

00:37:37.190 --> 00:37:43.490 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to say the
maximum over maybe j--

00:37:43.490 --> 00:37:46.340 align:middle line:90%
let me write it out explicitly--

00:37:46.340 --> 00:37:54.020 align:middle line:84%
of beta v2 all the
way up to beta vn.

00:37:54.020 --> 00:37:57.043 align:middle line:90%


00:37:57.043 --> 00:37:58.960 align:middle line:84%
I think, again, there's
always this confusion.

00:37:58.960 --> 00:38:01.880 align:middle line:84%
You see a probability, and then
you see b1 and people think,

00:38:01.880 --> 00:38:02.720 align:middle line:90%
b1 is random.

00:38:02.720 --> 00:38:05.020 align:middle line:84%
No, I know what b1 is.
b1 is just a number.

00:38:05.020 --> 00:38:08.452 align:middle line:84%
What I don't know is how much
my opponents are going to bid,

00:38:08.452 --> 00:38:10.660 align:middle line:84%
because even if I believe
they're using this strategy

00:38:10.660 --> 00:38:13.460 align:middle line:84%
beta, I don't know what
their actual valuations are.

00:38:13.460 --> 00:38:16.180 align:middle line:84%
And their actual valuations
affect how much they actually

00:38:16.180 --> 00:38:16.900 align:middle line:90%
bid.

00:38:16.900 --> 00:38:22.980 align:middle line:84%
So if I bid b1, I'm going to
win if player 2's bid is small,

00:38:22.980 --> 00:38:25.520 align:middle line:84%
player 3's bid is small,
player n's bid is small,

00:38:25.520 --> 00:38:27.020 align:middle line:84%
all of their bids
are small enough.

00:38:27.020 --> 00:38:28.860 align:middle line:84%
That is, the maximum
of all their bids

00:38:28.860 --> 00:38:31.060 align:middle line:90%
is smaller than my bid.

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:32.460 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:38:32.460 --> 00:38:35.833 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because beta
is strictly increasing?

00:38:35.833 --> 00:38:38.000 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Here, I'm actually
about to use that fact.

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:41.580 align:middle line:84%
I don't actually
need to use it here.

00:38:41.580 --> 00:38:44.680 align:middle line:84%
So I guess this statement
doesn't use the fact

00:38:44.680 --> 00:38:46.400 align:middle line:90%
that beta is increasing.

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:50.160 align:middle line:84%
Where that will come in is,
when will beta of v2 be small?

00:38:50.160 --> 00:38:52.480 align:middle line:90%
It will be small if v2 is small.

00:38:52.480 --> 00:38:54.100 align:middle line:84%
That's where
increasingness comes in.

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:55.960 align:middle line:84%
If beta were not
increasing, then maybe

00:38:55.960 --> 00:38:58.640 align:middle line:84%
I'd win when my opponent's value
was really high because that's

00:38:58.640 --> 00:38:59.560 align:middle line:90%
when they bid low.

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:01.040 align:middle line:84%
So there will be a step
where we use the fact

00:39:01.040 --> 00:39:02.180 align:middle line:90%
that beta is increasing.

00:39:02.180 --> 00:39:03.560 align:middle line:84%
But for now, we actually
haven't used that fact.

00:39:03.560 --> 00:39:06.192 align:middle line:84%
Because I win if my bid is
higher than everyone else's bid,

00:39:06.192 --> 00:39:08.400 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't matter whether
their bidding functions are

00:39:08.400 --> 00:39:12.140 align:middle line:84%
increasing or decreasing or
crazy, but that will come up.

00:39:12.140 --> 00:39:13.440 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:14.480 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:18.500 align:middle line:84%
So now we will exactly
go the next step.

00:39:18.500 --> 00:39:20.960 align:middle line:84%
Let's analyze this
probability here.

00:39:20.960 --> 00:39:28.200 align:middle line:84%
Well, since beta is increasing,
which of these players

00:39:28.200 --> 00:39:29.972 align:middle line:90%
is going to bid the most?

00:39:29.972 --> 00:39:31.680 align:middle line:84%
Well, beta is increasing,
and they're all

00:39:31.680 --> 00:39:33.237 align:middle line:90%
using the same bidding function.

00:39:33.237 --> 00:39:35.320 align:middle line:84%
So which of my opponents
is going to bid the most?

00:39:35.320 --> 00:39:35.840 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:39:35.840 --> 00:39:37.500 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The opponent
that has the highest value.

00:39:37.500 --> 00:39:38.740 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: The opponent
who has the highest value.

00:39:38.740 --> 00:39:40.940 align:middle line:84%
So actually, I don't need
to go all the way down.

00:39:40.940 --> 00:39:42.220 align:middle line:90%
Let me just go here.

00:39:42.220 --> 00:39:44.380 align:middle line:84%
I can simplify this
expression as just

00:39:44.380 --> 00:39:51.200 align:middle line:84%
writing this is beta of
the maximum of v2 up to vn.

00:39:51.200 --> 00:39:55.260 align:middle line:90%


00:39:55.260 --> 00:39:58.260 align:middle line:84%
And here, I am using the
fact that beta is increasing,

00:39:58.260 --> 00:40:01.340 align:middle line:84%
because I'm saying the player
who bids the most is exactly

00:40:01.340 --> 00:40:04.340 align:middle line:84%
going to be the player who
values the good the most.

00:40:04.340 --> 00:40:08.900 align:middle line:84%
So in words, this is the maximum
of the bids of my opponents.

00:40:08.900 --> 00:40:11.260 align:middle line:84%
In words, this is the
bid of my opponent

00:40:11.260 --> 00:40:12.832 align:middle line:90%
who has the highest value.

00:40:12.832 --> 00:40:14.540 align:middle line:84%
And because beta is
increasing, these two

00:40:14.540 --> 00:40:17.980 align:middle line:90%
are going to be the same.

00:40:17.980 --> 00:40:19.300 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:40:19.300 --> 00:40:21.640 align:middle line:84%
Now we can simplify
it a little bit more.

00:40:21.640 --> 00:40:24.580 align:middle line:90%


00:40:24.580 --> 00:40:27.560 align:middle line:84%
This is the random thing,
and we want to focus on that.

00:40:27.560 --> 00:40:29.760 align:middle line:84%
So let's apply beta
inverse to this.

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:34.900 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going to get is v1
minus b1 times the probability

00:40:34.900 --> 00:40:40.440 align:middle line:84%
that beta inverse of
b1 is greater than.

00:40:40.440 --> 00:40:48.462 align:middle line:90%


00:40:48.462 --> 00:40:49.920 align:middle line:84%
So one way of
thinking beta inverse

00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:53.560 align:middle line:84%
is saying, OK, let's
suppose I bid b1.

00:40:53.560 --> 00:41:00.720 align:middle line:90%
What is the valuation that--

00:41:00.720 --> 00:41:06.360 align:middle line:84%
which valuation using bidding
function beta would bid b1?

00:41:06.360 --> 00:41:09.280 align:middle line:84%
And we're saying that valuation
should be higher than everyone

00:41:09.280 --> 00:41:11.760 align:middle line:90%
else's valuations.

00:41:11.760 --> 00:41:15.080 align:middle line:84%
But mathematically, we're
just taking an inverse

00:41:15.080 --> 00:41:17.160 align:middle line:84%
So now we've simplified
things a bit.

00:41:17.160 --> 00:41:19.400 align:middle line:84%
I think one key
observation to point out

00:41:19.400 --> 00:41:22.220 align:middle line:84%
is the math has gotten a
little more complicated,

00:41:22.220 --> 00:41:24.400 align:middle line:84%
but the basic trade-off
hasn't changed.

00:41:24.400 --> 00:41:27.600 align:middle line:84%
When I bid more,
when I increase b1,

00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:30.980 align:middle line:84%
the first term gets
smaller, because when I win,

00:41:30.980 --> 00:41:34.240 align:middle line:84%
I win less because
I have to pay more.

00:41:34.240 --> 00:41:36.700 align:middle line:84%
What happens to the second
term if beta 1 goes up?

00:41:36.700 --> 00:41:40.340 align:middle line:90%


00:41:40.340 --> 00:41:42.420 align:middle line:84%
It's actually easier
to see maybe here.

00:41:42.420 --> 00:41:44.780 align:middle line:84%
If beta 1 goes up,
this increases.

00:41:44.780 --> 00:41:47.800 align:middle line:84%
Because if I bid more, it's
more likely that I win.

00:41:47.800 --> 00:41:50.360 align:middle line:84%
It's more likely that my bid
is higher than everyone else's.

00:41:50.360 --> 00:41:52.740 align:middle line:84%
So again, we face the basic
trade-off, if I bid more,

00:41:52.740 --> 00:41:54.552 align:middle line:84%
I increase the
chance that I win,

00:41:54.552 --> 00:41:55.760 align:middle line:90%
but I win less when I do win.

00:41:55.760 --> 00:41:58.700 align:middle line:84%
And we have to balance
those two effects.

00:41:58.700 --> 00:42:00.540 align:middle line:84%
So we're almost
there, but now we

00:42:00.540 --> 00:42:02.460 align:middle line:90%
need to analyze this expression.

00:42:02.460 --> 00:42:04.020 align:middle line:84%
And what we need
to do is we need

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:06.420 align:middle line:84%
to understand the
distribution of the maximum

00:42:06.420 --> 00:42:07.980 align:middle line:90%
of these random variables.

00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:11.040 align:middle line:84%
So this is where we're going
to use a little bit of math.

00:42:11.040 --> 00:42:13.780 align:middle line:84%
We basically need to compute,
what is the distribution

00:42:13.780 --> 00:42:15.940 align:middle line:90%
function of this maximum?

00:42:15.940 --> 00:42:22.660 align:middle line:84%
So we'll define let's say G of
v is equal to the probability

00:42:22.660 --> 00:42:28.740 align:middle line:84%
that the maximum of v1
up to vn is less than

00:42:28.740 --> 00:42:31.860 align:middle line:84%
or equal to v. This
is a definition.

00:42:31.860 --> 00:42:34.605 align:middle line:84%
And we want to try to
understand this function G.

00:42:34.605 --> 00:42:36.230 align:middle line:84%
Well, what does it
mean for the maximum

00:42:36.230 --> 00:42:39.590 align:middle line:84%
of these random variables to
be less than or equal to v?

00:42:39.590 --> 00:42:43.122 align:middle line:84%
Well, that's the same as
saying v1 is less than v--

00:42:43.122 --> 00:42:44.330 align:middle line:90%
ah, I shouldn't have v1 here.

00:42:44.330 --> 00:42:45.080 align:middle line:90%
This should be v2.

00:42:45.080 --> 00:42:47.590 align:middle line:90%
I apologize.

00:42:47.590 --> 00:42:50.670 align:middle line:84%
It's the same as saying v2 is
less than v, v3 is less than v,

00:42:50.670 --> 00:42:52.487 align:middle line:84%
all the way up to
vn is less than v.

00:42:52.487 --> 00:42:55.070 align:middle line:84%
For the maximum of things to be
smaller than something, that's

00:42:55.070 --> 00:42:56.987 align:middle line:84%
going to be true exactly
if every single thing

00:42:56.987 --> 00:43:00.950 align:middle line:84%
in the maximum is
smaller than that thing.

00:43:00.950 --> 00:43:06.470 align:middle line:84%
And the trick here is that
we can use independence.

00:43:06.470 --> 00:43:09.670 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out this is the
equal to the probability

00:43:09.670 --> 00:43:13.510 align:middle line:84%
that v2 is less than or equal
to v times all the way up

00:43:13.510 --> 00:43:18.790 align:middle line:84%
to the probability that vn
is less than or equal to v,

00:43:18.790 --> 00:43:21.350 align:middle line:90%
because this is the--

00:43:21.350 --> 00:43:26.070 align:middle line:84%
this occurs if and only if
each of these events occurs.

00:43:26.070 --> 00:43:28.070 align:middle line:84%
And because they're
independent, the probability

00:43:28.070 --> 00:43:29.310 align:middle line:84%
of the joint
occurrence of things

00:43:29.310 --> 00:43:30.970 align:middle line:84%
is just the product
of the probabilities.

00:43:30.970 --> 00:43:32.428 align:middle line:84%
This is like saying
the probability

00:43:32.428 --> 00:43:34.670 align:middle line:84%
that I flip two heads is
just one half times a half.

00:43:34.670 --> 00:43:35.490 align:middle line:90%
It's one quarter.

00:43:35.490 --> 00:43:38.530 align:middle line:84%
That's the fact that we're
using if they're independent.

00:43:38.530 --> 00:43:39.922 align:middle line:84%
And again, I feel
like whenever I

00:43:39.922 --> 00:43:41.630 align:middle line:84%
go over this, if you've
seen this before,

00:43:41.630 --> 00:43:42.547 align:middle line:90%
this will be too slow.

00:43:42.547 --> 00:43:45.250 align:middle line:84%
If you haven't seen
it, it'll be too fast.

00:43:45.250 --> 00:43:48.850 align:middle line:84%
I think there's not a
good solution to that.

00:43:48.850 --> 00:43:51.850 align:middle line:84%
Can I simplify this
expression on the right side?

00:43:51.850 --> 00:43:53.830 align:middle line:84%
Let's just be clear
what my notation means.

00:43:53.830 --> 00:43:55.690 align:middle line:84%
I have n minus 1
numbers here, and I'm

00:43:55.690 --> 00:43:56.950 align:middle line:90%
taking the product of them.

00:43:56.950 --> 00:43:58.970 align:middle line:90%
This is one number.

00:43:58.970 --> 00:44:01.930 align:middle line:84%
Then I have a number for v3, a
number for v4, all the way up

00:44:01.930 --> 00:44:02.470 align:middle line:90%
to vn.

00:44:02.470 --> 00:44:04.630 align:middle line:84%
I have n minus 1 numbers and
I'm multiplying them together.

00:44:04.630 --> 00:44:05.130 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:44:05.130 --> 00:44:09.708 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So isn't that just
the CDF at b to the n minus 1?

00:44:09.708 --> 00:44:10.750 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly, right?

00:44:10.750 --> 00:44:12.650 align:middle line:84%
Because what is the
probability that v2

00:44:12.650 --> 00:44:14.170 align:middle line:90%
is less than or equal to v?

00:44:14.170 --> 00:44:18.810 align:middle line:84%
Well, that's just F of
v. And the probability

00:44:18.810 --> 00:44:23.130 align:middle line:84%
that v3 is less than or equal
to v, that's just F of v.

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:25.770 align:middle line:84%
So I just get F of v
multiplied by itself

00:44:25.770 --> 00:44:27.970 align:middle line:84%
n minus 1 times
because I'm going

00:44:27.970 --> 00:44:30.010 align:middle line:90%
from 2 all the way up to n.

00:44:30.010 --> 00:44:36.130 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going to get
is F of v to the n minus 1.

00:44:36.130 --> 00:44:40.550 align:middle line:90%


00:44:40.550 --> 00:44:44.150 align:middle line:84%
Let's just make sure
this makes sense.

00:44:44.150 --> 00:44:48.550 align:middle line:84%
Is G of v going to be bigger
or smaller than F of v?

00:44:48.550 --> 00:44:52.070 align:middle line:84%
And let's see if
this makes sense.

00:44:52.070 --> 00:44:55.070 align:middle line:84%
So F of v is a number
between 0 and 1.

00:44:55.070 --> 00:44:56.950 align:middle line:84%
So when we multiply a
number between-- when

00:44:56.950 --> 00:45:00.230 align:middle line:84%
we raise a number between 0
and 1 to the power n minus 1,

00:45:00.230 --> 00:45:01.790 align:middle line:84%
that number is going
to get smaller.

00:45:01.790 --> 00:45:03.950 align:middle line:84%
And that makes sense
because the probability

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:06.390 align:middle line:84%
for all of these things
to be less than v

00:45:06.390 --> 00:45:09.510 align:middle line:84%
is certainly-- that's less
likely than the probability

00:45:09.510 --> 00:45:12.110 align:middle line:84%
that a single one of them
is less than or equal to v.

00:45:12.110 --> 00:45:14.850 align:middle line:84%
And we can also see another
intuitive property of this.

00:45:14.850 --> 00:45:16.890 align:middle line:84%
What happens when n
gets really, really big?

00:45:16.890 --> 00:45:19.630 align:middle line:90%


00:45:19.630 --> 00:45:22.190 align:middle line:84%
What happens to this
right-hand side?

00:45:22.190 --> 00:45:22.750 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:45:22.750 --> 00:45:26.470 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Lower probability
that you're on [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:26.470 --> 00:45:28.990 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Yeah, so it
gets really, really small,

00:45:28.990 --> 00:45:32.050 align:middle line:84%
because the probability that
everyone else is less than v

00:45:32.050 --> 00:45:33.030 align:middle line:90%
goes down.

00:45:33.030 --> 00:45:36.130 align:middle line:84%
So I just want to show this
kind of makes intuitive sense.

00:45:36.130 --> 00:45:41.450 align:middle line:84%
And then one final piece of
notation that will be helpful

00:45:41.450 --> 00:45:44.730 align:middle line:90%
is let me--

00:45:44.730 --> 00:45:45.870 align:middle line:90%
I'm out of space here.

00:45:45.870 --> 00:45:47.270 align:middle line:90%
Let me go to a new board.

00:45:47.270 --> 00:45:58.890 align:middle line:90%


00:45:58.890 --> 00:46:01.630 align:middle line:84%
Actually, realize it'll be
good to be close to this board.

00:46:01.630 --> 00:46:03.190 align:middle line:84%
So let's go to
this board instead.

00:46:03.190 --> 00:46:25.710 align:middle line:90%


00:46:25.710 --> 00:46:29.070 align:middle line:84%
So I just wanted to
find the density,

00:46:29.070 --> 00:46:33.550 align:middle line:84%
the probability density
function, associated with G.

00:46:33.550 --> 00:46:40.390 align:middle line:84%
So let's define one other
definition, little g of v

00:46:40.390 --> 00:46:44.590 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
derivative of big G.

00:46:44.590 --> 00:46:47.150 align:middle line:84%
Well, if big G equals
this, now I just

00:46:47.150 --> 00:46:50.710 align:middle line:84%
have to apply the
chain rule, and I'll

00:46:50.710 --> 00:47:01.170 align:middle line:84%
get n minus 1 times F of v to
the n minus 2 times f of v.

00:47:01.170 --> 00:47:04.772 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just using the power rule
to bring the n minus 1 down,

00:47:04.772 --> 00:47:06.230 align:middle line:84%
and then the chain
rule to multiply

00:47:06.230 --> 00:47:09.830 align:middle line:84%
by little f using the
fact that little f is

00:47:09.830 --> 00:47:14.090 align:middle line:84%
the derivative of big F. If
you're a little rusty on this,

00:47:14.090 --> 00:47:17.170 align:middle line:90%
that's not so important.

00:47:17.170 --> 00:47:19.470 align:middle line:84%
So with all these
simplifications,

00:47:19.470 --> 00:47:23.170 align:middle line:84%
I think we now have a
pretty clean formula.

00:47:23.170 --> 00:47:35.200 align:middle line:84%
We have U1 of b1 given v1 beta
is equal to v1 minus b1 times--

00:47:35.200 --> 00:47:46.520 align:middle line:84%
well, we use our function G, And
we get G of beta inverse of b1.

00:47:46.520 --> 00:47:50.440 align:middle line:84%
So all I've done is used our
notation G to apply to here.

00:47:50.440 --> 00:47:53.240 align:middle line:84%
There's a little wrinkle because
this was a strict inequality

00:47:53.240 --> 00:47:56.140 align:middle line:84%
and big G is defined
by a weak inequality.

00:47:56.140 --> 00:47:58.913 align:middle line:84%
But the probability that the
maximum takes a single value

00:47:58.913 --> 00:48:00.580 align:middle line:84%
is 0, so that doesn't
make a difference,

00:48:00.580 --> 00:48:03.300 align:middle line:90%
so I can ignore that.

00:48:03.300 --> 00:48:06.640 align:middle line:84%
So now this is the expression
we want to maximize, once again

00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:09.040 align:middle line:84%
recognizing the trade-off
that as I increase b1,

00:48:09.040 --> 00:48:11.320 align:middle line:84%
this gets smaller
and this gets bigger.

00:48:11.320 --> 00:48:14.420 align:middle line:84%
So in words, this is the
probability that I win.

00:48:14.420 --> 00:48:20.825 align:middle line:90%


00:48:20.825 --> 00:48:23.200 align:middle line:84%
The way we're expressing it
is the probability that I win

00:48:23.200 --> 00:48:26.340 align:middle line:84%
is the probability that
everyone else's valuation is

00:48:26.340 --> 00:48:28.700 align:middle line:90%
below this magical threshold.

00:48:28.700 --> 00:48:30.278 align:middle line:84%
What's magical about
this threshold?

00:48:30.278 --> 00:48:31.820 align:middle line:84%
This is the valuation
that they would

00:48:31.820 --> 00:48:35.060 align:middle line:90%
have to have to bid exactly b1.

00:48:35.060 --> 00:48:37.980 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to win if every
single player's valuation is

00:48:37.980 --> 00:48:41.080 align:middle line:84%
below the valuation that they
would need in order to bid b1.

00:48:41.080 --> 00:48:44.780 align:middle line:90%


00:48:44.780 --> 00:48:47.620 align:middle line:84%
So now what I want
to do is I want

00:48:47.620 --> 00:48:49.460 align:middle line:84%
to take the derivative
of this expression

00:48:49.460 --> 00:48:51.600 align:middle line:84%
so that I can choose
the b1 optimally.

00:48:51.600 --> 00:48:53.000 align:middle line:84%
I want to maximize
this function.

00:48:53.000 --> 00:48:54.900 align:middle line:84%
This function is telling
me what my payoff

00:48:54.900 --> 00:48:57.620 align:middle line:84%
is as a function
of how much I bid.

00:48:57.620 --> 00:49:00.620 align:middle line:84%
So let me just take
derivatives and maximize this.

00:49:00.620 --> 00:49:03.740 align:middle line:84%
So if I take derivatives
and evaluate the first-order

00:49:03.740 --> 00:49:07.000 align:middle line:84%
condition, I'm
going to get minus--

00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:31.600 align:middle line:90%


00:49:31.600 --> 00:49:33.280 align:middle line:90%
Let's go through what I've done.

00:49:33.280 --> 00:49:35.280 align:middle line:84%
I've taken the derivative
of the first term

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:40.560 align:middle line:84%
is negative 1 and multiplied it
by the second term to get this.

00:49:40.560 --> 00:49:42.960 align:middle line:84%
Then I've done the first
term times the derivative

00:49:42.960 --> 00:49:44.200 align:middle line:90%
of the second term.

00:49:44.200 --> 00:49:45.680 align:middle line:90%
I've used the chain rule.

00:49:45.680 --> 00:49:47.640 align:middle line:84%
So when I take the
derivative of this,

00:49:47.640 --> 00:49:51.720 align:middle line:84%
first I take the derivative
of big G to get little g.

00:49:51.720 --> 00:49:54.360 align:middle line:84%
And then I need to take the
derivative of beta inverse.

00:49:54.360 --> 00:49:57.280 align:middle line:84%
But here I'm using what's called
the implicit function theorem.

00:49:57.280 --> 00:50:00.357 align:middle line:84%
The derivative of the inverse is
the inverse of the derivative.

00:50:00.357 --> 00:50:01.940 align:middle line:84%
It's the reciprocal
of the derivative.

00:50:01.940 --> 00:50:05.160 align:middle line:90%
So this is just a calculus fact.

00:50:05.160 --> 00:50:06.680 align:middle line:84%
The slope of the
inverse is just 1

00:50:06.680 --> 00:50:08.680 align:middle line:84%
over the slope of the
original function.

00:50:08.680 --> 00:50:11.140 align:middle line:90%
So I'm going to get this here.

00:50:11.140 --> 00:50:19.846 align:middle line:90%


00:50:19.846 --> 00:50:26.460 align:middle line:84%
So now I have a formula for what
my optimal bid b1 should be.

00:50:26.460 --> 00:50:30.620 align:middle line:84%
So my optimal bid b1 has
to satisfy this formula.

00:50:30.620 --> 00:50:34.820 align:middle line:84%
But I'm looking
for an equilibrium.

00:50:34.820 --> 00:50:42.580 align:middle line:84%
So it must be the case that
this holds when b1 equals.

00:50:42.580 --> 00:50:44.460 align:middle line:90%
beta of V1.

00:50:44.460 --> 00:50:46.980 align:middle line:90%
This is kind of a subtle point.

00:50:46.980 --> 00:50:48.940 align:middle line:84%
Let's understand what
we're doing here.

00:50:48.940 --> 00:50:52.780 align:middle line:84%
This is a formula that's
satisfied by my optimal bid.

00:50:52.780 --> 00:50:55.460 align:middle line:84%
But in order for this
to be an equilibrium,

00:50:55.460 --> 00:51:00.323 align:middle line:84%
my optimal bid
must be beta of v1,

00:51:00.323 --> 00:51:02.740 align:middle line:84%
because we want it to be the
case that if everyone else is

00:51:02.740 --> 00:51:06.340 align:middle line:84%
using this bidding function
beta, and my valuation is v1,

00:51:06.340 --> 00:51:11.100 align:middle line:84%
then the optimal bid for
me is exactly beta of v1.

00:51:11.100 --> 00:51:13.800 align:middle line:84%
So we know that this
must hold at beta v1.

00:51:13.800 --> 00:51:16.320 align:middle line:84%
So maybe a more formal
way of saying this,

00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:17.540 align:middle line:90%
so why is this true?

00:51:17.540 --> 00:51:19.600 align:middle line:90%
Let me give the justification.

00:51:19.600 --> 00:51:24.440 align:middle line:84%
It's because beta of
v1 must be an element

00:51:24.440 --> 00:51:29.540 align:middle line:90%
of the argmax over b1 prime.

00:51:29.540 --> 00:51:36.680 align:middle line:90%


00:51:36.680 --> 00:51:38.360 align:middle line:84%
So of all the
possible bids I could

00:51:38.360 --> 00:51:42.040 align:middle line:84%
choose as player 1, following
my equilibrium strategy

00:51:42.040 --> 00:51:44.160 align:middle line:90%
must be the best possible bid.

00:51:44.160 --> 00:51:45.720 align:middle line:84%
So when I take
derivatives of this

00:51:45.720 --> 00:51:47.840 align:middle line:84%
and set them to 0
to find the maximum,

00:51:47.840 --> 00:51:50.200 align:middle line:84%
that first-order condition
must be satisfied

00:51:50.200 --> 00:51:53.400 align:middle line:90%
by my maximizer, beta of v1.

00:51:53.400 --> 00:51:55.332 align:middle line:84%
I think this is maybe
a little subtle.

00:51:55.332 --> 00:51:56.540 align:middle line:90%
This is kind of the key step.

00:51:56.540 --> 00:52:00.200 align:middle line:90%
So any questions here?

00:52:00.200 --> 00:52:00.960 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:52:00.960 --> 00:52:02.710 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So are you
basically just saying

00:52:02.710 --> 00:52:06.020 align:middle line:84%
that this is only necessarily
true if b1 equals beta?

00:52:06.020 --> 00:52:07.020 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:52:07.020 --> 00:52:07.520 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:52:07.520 --> 00:52:11.320 align:middle line:84%
So I'm solving for the optimal--
this is a condition that must

00:52:11.320 --> 00:52:15.160 align:middle line:90%
be satisfied by my optimal bid.

00:52:15.160 --> 00:52:18.870 align:middle line:84%
But then I know that beta of
v1 must be my optimal bid.

00:52:18.870 --> 00:52:22.750 align:middle line:84%
So therefore, beta of v1
must satisfy this equation.

00:52:22.750 --> 00:52:24.750 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to
simplify things a lot right.

00:52:24.750 --> 00:52:29.310 align:middle line:84%
So let me plug in beta of
v1 and see what I get here.

00:52:29.310 --> 00:52:33.610 align:middle line:90%


00:52:33.610 --> 00:52:36.350 align:middle line:84%
And of course, the details
of this are all in the notes,

00:52:36.350 --> 00:52:39.950 align:middle line:84%
but I think if you don't
see it in real time,

00:52:39.950 --> 00:52:41.770 align:middle line:84%
it can get a little
overwhelming.

00:52:41.770 --> 00:52:46.690 align:middle line:90%


00:52:46.690 --> 00:52:50.698 align:middle line:84%
So if we plug in
beta of v1, we're

00:52:50.698 --> 00:52:52.490 align:middle line:84%
actually going to get--
things are actually

00:52:52.490 --> 00:52:54.650 align:middle line:90%
going to simplify a lot.

00:52:54.650 --> 00:53:03.850 align:middle line:84%
We're going to get 0
equals negative G of v1,

00:53:03.850 --> 00:53:08.450 align:middle line:84%
because beta inverse of
beta of v1 is just v1.

00:53:08.450 --> 00:53:25.110 align:middle line:84%
And then we get plus v1 minus
beta of v1 times g of v1 times--

00:53:25.110 --> 00:53:30.650 align:middle line:90%
divided by beta prime of v1.

00:53:30.650 --> 00:53:33.390 align:middle line:90%


00:53:33.390 --> 00:53:35.430 align:middle line:84%
So this is a much
simpler formula.

00:53:35.430 --> 00:53:40.990 align:middle line:84%
And we can actually simplify
it a little further.

00:53:40.990 --> 00:53:43.670 align:middle line:84%
We know we're allowed to divide
by this because beta prime is

00:53:43.670 --> 00:53:45.190 align:middle line:90%
strictly positive.

00:53:45.190 --> 00:53:47.230 align:middle line:84%
That's an assumption
we made over here.

00:53:47.230 --> 00:53:51.230 align:middle line:84%
So let's multiply by beta prime
and move this to the other side.

00:53:51.230 --> 00:53:59.890 align:middle line:84%
And what we get is beta
prime of v1, G of v1 equals--

00:53:59.890 --> 00:54:14.770 align:middle line:90%


00:54:14.770 --> 00:54:18.250 align:middle line:84%
and this is a
necessary condition

00:54:18.250 --> 00:54:21.370 align:middle line:84%
for-- that our beta function
must satisfy in order for us

00:54:21.370 --> 00:54:25.690 align:middle line:84%
to have an equilibrium where
everyone uses the beta function.

00:54:25.690 --> 00:54:30.890 align:middle line:84%
Now, I think we can actually
intuit this pretty nicely.

00:54:30.890 --> 00:54:33.850 align:middle line:84%
So here's some nice
intuition for this.

00:54:33.850 --> 00:54:38.610 align:middle line:84%
What I want to say is
suppose my value is v1,

00:54:38.610 --> 00:54:42.370 align:middle line:84%
and I'm contemplating,
instead of bidding beta of v1,

00:54:42.370 --> 00:54:45.390 align:middle line:84%
I could bid beta
of v1 plus epsilon.

00:54:45.390 --> 00:54:50.450 align:middle line:90%


00:54:50.450 --> 00:54:53.730 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's think about
epsilon being very small.

00:54:53.730 --> 00:54:56.890 align:middle line:84%
So I'm supposed
to bid beta of v1.

00:54:56.890 --> 00:54:58.570 align:middle line:84%
We want to see what
happens if I instead

00:54:58.570 --> 00:55:00.530 align:middle line:90%
bid beta of v1 plus epsilon.

00:55:00.530 --> 00:55:06.410 align:middle line:84%
And this is approximately going
to be beta of v1 plus epsilon

00:55:06.410 --> 00:55:10.090 align:middle line:90%
times b prime of v1.

00:55:10.090 --> 00:55:11.930 align:middle line:90%
This is just a Taylor expansion.

00:55:11.930 --> 00:55:16.710 align:middle line:90%


00:55:16.710 --> 00:55:25.990 align:middle line:84%
So if epsilon is really small
and positive, what I claim

00:55:25.990 --> 00:55:44.070 align:middle line:84%
is that this is measuring
the gain from winning more.

00:55:44.070 --> 00:55:53.070 align:middle line:84%
And this is measuring
the additional payment

00:55:53.070 --> 00:55:55.050 align:middle line:90%
to understand what's going on.

00:55:55.050 --> 00:56:00.710 align:middle line:90%


00:56:00.710 --> 00:56:07.790 align:middle line:84%
If I deviate from beta of v1
to beta of v1 plus epsilon,

00:56:07.790 --> 00:56:11.658 align:middle line:90%
then I'm going to win--

00:56:11.658 --> 00:56:13.450 align:middle line:84%
how is that going to
change the probability

00:56:13.450 --> 00:56:15.370 align:middle line:90%
that I win the auction?

00:56:15.370 --> 00:56:28.730 align:middle line:84%
Well it's only going to make
a difference if my opponents--

00:56:28.730 --> 00:56:30.810 align:middle line:84%
if the maximum
value of my opponent

00:56:30.810 --> 00:56:35.370 align:middle line:84%
is between v1 and
v1 plus epsilon.

00:56:35.370 --> 00:56:37.250 align:middle line:84%
If the maximum value
of my opponents

00:56:37.250 --> 00:56:39.490 align:middle line:84%
is between v1 and
v1 plus epsilon,

00:56:39.490 --> 00:56:42.590 align:middle line:84%
then when I bid as if my
evaluation were v1, I lost.

00:56:42.590 --> 00:56:46.070 align:middle line:84%
But when I increase my bid to
bidding beta v1 plus epsilon,

00:56:46.070 --> 00:56:46.830 align:middle line:90%
now I win.

00:56:46.830 --> 00:56:51.850 align:middle line:90%


00:56:51.850 --> 00:56:59.530 align:middle line:84%
The probability of that is
roughly g of v1 times epsilon,

00:56:59.530 --> 00:57:02.490 align:middle line:84%
because it's the density
of my opponents' bid

00:57:02.490 --> 00:57:04.890 align:middle line:84%
times the width
of this interval.

00:57:04.890 --> 00:57:08.580 align:middle line:84%
So what this right-hand
term is exactly

00:57:08.580 --> 00:57:11.340 align:middle line:84%
expressing is it's saying if
I slightly increase my bid,

00:57:11.340 --> 00:57:15.100 align:middle line:84%
I get my utility if I win times
a slightly higher probability

00:57:15.100 --> 00:57:18.600 align:middle line:84%
of winning, which is exactly
g of v1 times epsilon.

00:57:18.600 --> 00:57:21.220 align:middle line:84%
That's the amount by which my
winning probability goes up

00:57:21.220 --> 00:57:22.820 align:middle line:90%
by bidding a bit more.

00:57:22.820 --> 00:57:27.220 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, if I
bid a little bit more,

00:57:27.220 --> 00:57:32.220 align:middle line:84%
I have to pay epsilon beta
prime of v1 whenever I win.

00:57:32.220 --> 00:57:34.740 align:middle line:84%
So I lose this much
in additional payments

00:57:34.740 --> 00:57:35.397 align:middle line:90%
whenever I win.

00:57:35.397 --> 00:57:36.980 align:middle line:84%
And what's the
probability that I win?

00:57:36.980 --> 00:57:38.400 align:middle line:90%
Exactly big G of v1.

00:57:38.400 --> 00:57:39.493 align:middle line:90%
So there's a very--

00:57:39.493 --> 00:57:41.160 align:middle line:84%
if you could really
had great intuition,

00:57:41.160 --> 00:57:43.260 align:middle line:84%
you could maybe jump
right away to this formula

00:57:43.260 --> 00:57:46.120 align:middle line:84%
by thinking through this, but
we derived it with calculus,

00:57:46.120 --> 00:57:47.280 align:middle line:90%
so it doesn't matter.

00:57:47.280 --> 00:57:49.860 align:middle line:90%


00:57:49.860 --> 00:57:52.580 align:middle line:84%
So to be very precise,
what I'm saying

00:57:52.580 --> 00:57:55.320 align:middle line:84%
is if instead of
bidding beta of v1,

00:57:55.320 --> 00:57:57.780 align:middle line:84%
I bid beta of v1
plus epsilon, then

00:57:57.780 --> 00:58:00.580 align:middle line:84%
my expected additional payment
would be approximately epsilon

00:58:00.580 --> 00:58:04.220 align:middle line:84%
times the left-hand side, and my
expected gain from winning more

00:58:04.220 --> 00:58:07.240 align:middle line:84%
would be approximately epsilon
times the right-hand side.

00:58:07.240 --> 00:58:09.360 align:middle line:84%
And if I'm already
in an optimum,

00:58:09.360 --> 00:58:13.060 align:middle line:84%
then the effect of this
perturbation must be 0.

00:58:13.060 --> 00:58:14.620 align:middle line:84%
This is like a
first-order condition.

00:58:14.620 --> 00:58:16.078 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, I
get this equation.

00:58:16.078 --> 00:58:18.340 align:middle line:84%
But that may have
been a little subtle.

00:58:18.340 --> 00:58:20.080 align:middle line:84%
So that's how I like
to think about it.

00:58:20.080 --> 00:58:22.580 align:middle line:84%
But if you don't see that the
first time, I think that's OK.

00:58:22.580 --> 00:58:23.080 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:58:23.080 --> 00:58:26.760 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So the idea isn't
that in the formula, you've--

00:58:26.760 --> 00:58:30.220 align:middle line:84%
so you have to multiply
both sides by epsilon.

00:58:30.220 --> 00:58:30.926 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:58:30.926 --> 00:58:31.280 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:58:31.280 --> 00:58:32.800 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So epsilon
is an implicit.

00:58:32.800 --> 00:58:33.467 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Right.

00:58:33.467 --> 00:58:38.460 align:middle line:84%
So the argument is if I
multiply both sides by epsilon,

00:58:38.460 --> 00:58:42.663 align:middle line:84%
then I guess the more
formal argument would

00:58:42.663 --> 00:58:44.080 align:middle line:84%
be I could actually
choose epsilon

00:58:44.080 --> 00:58:47.218 align:middle line:84%
to be a small positive number
or a small negative number.

00:58:47.218 --> 00:58:49.760 align:middle line:84%
So because I can choose it to
be a small positive or negative

00:58:49.760 --> 00:58:51.280 align:middle line:84%
number, the only
way that I won't

00:58:51.280 --> 00:58:54.720 align:middle line:84%
have a profitable deviation is
if both sides of this are equal.

00:58:54.720 --> 00:58:56.520 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And then
why does it make sense

00:58:56.520 --> 00:58:59.960 align:middle line:84%
that some of this stuff is
like derivative, some of it's

00:58:59.960 --> 00:59:02.260 align:middle line:90%
like CDF, et cetera.

00:59:02.260 --> 00:59:05.460 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Yeah, so let's
understand the role of it.

00:59:05.460 --> 00:59:07.780 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying, what happens
if I bid slightly higher?

00:59:07.780 --> 00:59:09.420 align:middle line:90%
Let's take epsilon positive.

00:59:09.420 --> 00:59:13.380 align:middle line:84%
Well, the amount
more by which I bid

00:59:13.380 --> 00:59:15.480 align:middle line:84%
is epsilon times
beta prime of v1.

00:59:15.480 --> 00:59:16.860 align:middle line:90%
That's how much more I bid.

00:59:16.860 --> 00:59:19.620 align:middle line:84%
So you can think of that--
let's put the mini epsilon here.

00:59:19.620 --> 00:59:21.500 align:middle line:84%
That's how much
more I'm bidding.

00:59:21.500 --> 00:59:23.860 align:middle line:84%
But what's the increase
in how much I pay?

00:59:23.860 --> 00:59:26.820 align:middle line:84%
Well, I don't actually pay
that extra amount unless I win.

00:59:26.820 --> 00:59:28.340 align:middle line:84%
What's the probability
that I win?

00:59:28.340 --> 00:59:31.100 align:middle line:84%
It's the probability that
everyone else's value

00:59:31.100 --> 00:59:32.380 align:middle line:90%
is below mine.

00:59:32.380 --> 00:59:34.080 align:middle line:90%
That's exactly G of V1.

00:59:34.080 --> 00:59:36.700 align:middle line:84%
So this is the probability I
win times the extra payment I

00:59:36.700 --> 00:59:38.700 align:middle line:90%
make if I do win.

00:59:38.700 --> 00:59:43.300 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, I'm looking
at the extra probability

00:59:43.300 --> 00:59:47.760 align:middle line:90%
that I win is g times epsilon.

00:59:47.760 --> 00:59:49.260 align:middle line:90%
That's what I get down here.

00:59:49.260 --> 00:59:52.140 align:middle line:84%
But then that marginal
change in how much I win

00:59:52.140 --> 00:59:56.300 align:middle line:84%
is multiplied by the
utility I get when I do win.

00:59:56.300 --> 00:59:59.268 align:middle line:84%
But I'll admit this is a
little-- if you follow the math

00:59:59.268 --> 01:00:01.060 align:middle line:84%
and you don't fully
see all this intuition,

01:00:01.060 --> 01:00:02.500 align:middle line:84%
I think that's OK
the first time.

01:00:02.500 --> 01:00:04.460 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:00:04.460 --> 01:00:05.720 align:middle line:90%
So now we want to solve this.

01:00:05.720 --> 01:00:06.720 align:middle line:90%
We're close.

01:00:06.720 --> 01:00:08.420 align:middle line:90%
We have this formula.

01:00:08.420 --> 01:00:12.120 align:middle line:90%
Let me erase the epsilons now.

01:00:12.120 --> 01:00:13.800 align:middle line:84%
And now we have a
differential equation.

01:00:13.800 --> 01:00:15.175 align:middle line:84%
This is not really
a course where

01:00:15.175 --> 01:00:17.920 align:middle line:84%
we're trying to test how well
people can solve differential

01:00:17.920 --> 01:00:20.220 align:middle line:84%
equations, but this one,
there's a pretty nice trick.

01:00:20.220 --> 01:00:22.720 align:middle line:90%
So let me just--

01:00:22.720 --> 01:00:24.840 align:middle line:84%
I want to get all
the betas to one side

01:00:24.840 --> 01:00:27.100 align:middle line:84%
because we now have a
differential equation for beta.

01:00:27.100 --> 01:00:29.433 align:middle line:84%
And now we want to find the
function beta that satisfies

01:00:29.433 --> 01:00:30.760 align:middle line:90%
this differential equation.

01:00:30.760 --> 01:00:33.160 align:middle line:84%
So if we move all the
betas to one side--

01:00:33.160 --> 01:00:36.580 align:middle line:84%
and let me also call my
variable now v instead of v1,

01:00:36.580 --> 01:00:38.720 align:middle line:84%
just to make things--
life a little easier.

01:00:38.720 --> 01:00:46.440 align:middle line:84%
So then my differential equation
becomes beta prime of v G of v

01:00:46.440 --> 01:00:55.360 align:middle line:90%
plus beta of v g of v.

01:00:55.360 --> 01:00:56.940 align:middle line:84%
So this is my
differential equation.

01:00:56.940 --> 01:00:58.482 align:middle line:84%
So formally, what
we've done is we've

01:00:58.482 --> 01:01:00.580 align:middle line:84%
said-- we've guessed
that there exists

01:01:00.580 --> 01:01:05.300 align:middle line:84%
an equilibrium in which everyone
used the bidding function beta.

01:01:05.300 --> 01:01:07.980 align:middle line:84%
And then we've used the
properties of equilibrium

01:01:07.980 --> 01:01:09.780 align:middle line:84%
to argue that if
that's the case,

01:01:09.780 --> 01:01:12.280 align:middle line:84%
then we know a lot about what
this beta function must be.

01:01:12.280 --> 01:01:15.860 align:middle line:84%
In fact, it must satisfy this
special differential equation.

01:01:15.860 --> 01:01:18.600 align:middle line:84%
And now we want to solve for
this differential equation.

01:01:18.600 --> 01:01:20.410 align:middle line:84%
Find the function
that satisfies this.

01:01:20.410 --> 01:01:22.660 align:middle line:84%
And if anyone's recently
taken differential equations,

01:01:22.660 --> 01:01:25.557 align:middle line:84%
they may see there's
kind of something special

01:01:25.557 --> 01:01:26.640 align:middle line:90%
about this left-hand side.

01:01:26.640 --> 01:01:28.640 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone-- anything
pop out at you.

01:01:28.640 --> 01:01:32.260 align:middle line:84%
I don't know you just
took a class on ODEs?

01:01:32.260 --> 01:01:32.897 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:01:32.897 --> 01:01:34.980 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That looks like
the product [INAUDIBLE].

01:01:34.980 --> 01:01:35.740 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly.

01:01:35.740 --> 01:01:42.883 align:middle line:84%
So let's express the left-hand
side as beta of v G of v--

01:01:42.883 --> 01:01:45.300 align:middle line:84%
this is kind of sloppy notation,
but I mean the derivative

01:01:45.300 --> 01:01:46.133 align:middle line:90%
of this whole thing.

01:01:46.133 --> 01:01:47.298 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll write d/dv.

01:01:47.298 --> 01:01:47.840 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

01:01:47.840 --> 01:01:49.840 align:middle line:84%
Everyone has different
notation for derivatives.

01:01:49.840 --> 01:01:51.260 align:middle line:90%
Let's write that.

01:01:51.260 --> 01:01:53.220 align:middle line:84%
Well, now I have the
derivative of something,

01:01:53.220 --> 01:01:54.500 align:middle line:90%
and I have something else.

01:01:54.500 --> 01:01:57.560 align:middle line:84%
So now I'm just going
to integrate this from v

01:01:57.560 --> 01:02:03.260 align:middle line:84%
lower bar to v. There's always
an issue with what terms we use.

01:02:03.260 --> 01:02:07.513 align:middle line:84%
But, OK, so let's integrate
both sides from v lower bar to--

01:02:07.513 --> 01:02:10.180 align:middle line:84%
well, we don't want to call it v
because we already have v here.

01:02:10.180 --> 01:02:11.880 align:middle line:90%
So let's call it V hat

01:02:11.880 --> 01:02:14.800 align:middle line:84%
So on the left-hand
side, we get beta

01:02:14.800 --> 01:02:21.040 align:middle line:84%
of v G of v evaluated
from v equals v lower bar

01:02:21.040 --> 01:02:23.640 align:middle line:90%
to let's say V hat.

01:02:23.640 --> 01:02:27.360 align:middle line:84%
And on the right-hand side,
we get the integral from v

01:02:27.360 --> 01:02:36.200 align:middle line:84%
lower bar to V hat
of v g of v dv.

01:02:36.200 --> 01:02:39.320 align:middle line:84%
So if these two sides are
equal, certainly the integrals

01:02:39.320 --> 01:02:40.720 align:middle line:90%
of the two sides are equal.

01:02:40.720 --> 01:02:43.788 align:middle line:84%
I've integrated the right side
from v lower bar to v upper bar.

01:02:43.788 --> 01:02:46.080 align:middle line:84%
And I've also integrated the
left side from v lower bar

01:02:46.080 --> 01:02:47.020 align:middle line:90%
to v upper bar.

01:02:47.020 --> 01:02:48.760 align:middle line:84%
But this just takes
a really simple form

01:02:48.760 --> 01:02:51.180 align:middle line:84%
because I'm taking the
integral of a derivative.

01:02:51.180 --> 01:02:54.360 align:middle line:84%
The integral of a derivative
is just the antiderivative

01:02:54.360 --> 01:02:56.690 align:middle line:90%
at the endpoints.

01:02:56.690 --> 01:03:00.690 align:middle line:84%
And this notation here means
I plug in v equals V hat,

01:03:00.690 --> 01:03:03.210 align:middle line:84%
and then I subtract
the expression with v

01:03:03.210 --> 01:03:04.110 align:middle line:90%
equals v lower bar.

01:03:04.110 --> 01:03:07.570 align:middle line:84%
I think this is pretty
standard notation.

01:03:07.570 --> 01:03:11.470 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll put in arrows
and I'll write integrate.

01:03:11.470 --> 01:03:15.170 align:middle line:90%


01:03:15.170 --> 01:03:17.050 align:middle line:90%
So now we're getting closer.

01:03:17.050 --> 01:03:19.210 align:middle line:84%
But I'd argue that this
left-hand side simplifies

01:03:19.210 --> 01:03:20.050 align:middle line:90%
even more.

01:03:20.050 --> 01:03:22.050 align:middle line:84%
How can we simplify
this left-hand side?

01:03:22.050 --> 01:03:23.590 align:middle line:90%
What happens at v lower bar?

01:03:23.590 --> 01:03:26.450 align:middle line:90%


01:03:26.450 --> 01:03:26.970 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:03:26.970 --> 01:03:28.730 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: G of v lower bar is 0.

01:03:28.730 --> 01:03:30.230 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: G of v
lower bar is zero.

01:03:30.230 --> 01:03:31.563 align:middle line:90%
So we can just ignore that term.

01:03:31.563 --> 01:03:34.250 align:middle line:84%
That disappears, right, because
we get 0 times something.

01:03:34.250 --> 01:03:36.330 align:middle line:90%
So we only get the top term.

01:03:36.330 --> 01:03:42.730 align:middle line:84%
So we get beta of V hat
times G of V hat on the left

01:03:42.730 --> 01:03:45.170 align:middle line:84%
equals the integral
from v lower bar

01:03:45.170 --> 01:03:49.730 align:middle line:90%
to v upper bar of v g of v dv.

01:03:49.730 --> 01:03:51.730 align:middle line:84%
And now we've solved our
differential equation

01:03:51.730 --> 01:03:53.890 align:middle line:90%
because we have beta by itself.

01:03:53.890 --> 01:03:58.450 align:middle line:84%
And now we can just divide
by big G. So let's divide.

01:03:58.450 --> 01:04:15.310 align:middle line:90%


01:04:15.310 --> 01:04:22.050 align:middle line:84%
So we get our solution is beta--
and this big V is annoying.

01:04:22.050 --> 01:04:26.070 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll change notation a
little bit and write beta of v--

01:04:26.070 --> 01:04:28.470 align:middle line:84%
let me just use v
instead of V hat

01:04:28.470 --> 01:04:30.470 align:middle line:84%
and then maybe x
instead of v. So

01:04:30.470 --> 01:04:32.710 align:middle line:84%
let me say this is
the integral from v

01:04:32.710 --> 01:04:38.210 align:middle line:90%
lower bar to v of x g of x.

01:04:38.210 --> 01:04:42.070 align:middle line:90%


01:04:42.070 --> 01:04:43.990 align:middle line:90%
No.

01:04:43.990 --> 01:04:48.390 align:middle line:84%
So let me check wherever
I had a v hat I want a V.

01:04:48.390 --> 01:04:51.890 align:middle line:84%
And wherever I had a
little v I want an x.

01:04:51.890 --> 01:04:52.630 align:middle line:90%
Looks good.

01:04:52.630 --> 01:04:56.090 align:middle line:90%


01:04:56.090 --> 01:04:57.150 align:middle line:90%
So here's our solution.

01:04:57.150 --> 01:05:00.770 align:middle line:90%


01:05:00.770 --> 01:05:04.170 align:middle line:84%
So what we've solved for
is the bidding function

01:05:04.170 --> 01:05:06.510 align:middle line:84%
that every player
is going to use

01:05:06.510 --> 01:05:08.250 align:middle line:90%
in this symmetric equilibrium.

01:05:08.250 --> 01:05:09.170 align:middle line:90%
I should warn that--

01:05:09.170 --> 01:05:11.850 align:middle line:84%
I should caution that there's
one kind of trick we've done.

01:05:11.850 --> 01:05:15.450 align:middle line:84%
Technically, what we've
shown is that if there's

01:05:15.450 --> 01:05:19.933 align:middle line:84%
an equilibrium of this form,
then beta must look like this.

01:05:19.933 --> 01:05:22.350 align:middle line:84%
But really, we have to check
that if beta looks like this,

01:05:22.350 --> 01:05:23.558 align:middle line:90%
it's actually an equilibrium.

01:05:23.558 --> 01:05:25.290 align:middle line:84%
We've shown a necessary
condition, not

01:05:25.290 --> 01:05:26.470 align:middle line:90%
a sufficient condition.

01:05:26.470 --> 01:05:28.350 align:middle line:84%
But you can check that this
actually is an equilibrium,

01:05:28.350 --> 01:05:28.892 align:middle line:90%
so that's OK.

01:05:28.892 --> 01:05:31.290 align:middle line:84%
It's sort of like when you
solve a maximization problem

01:05:31.290 --> 01:05:32.932 align:middle line:84%
and you set the
derivative to 0, well,

01:05:32.932 --> 01:05:34.390 align:middle line:84%
sometimes you don't
have a maximum,

01:05:34.390 --> 01:05:35.490 align:middle line:90%
you have a minimum or something.

01:05:35.490 --> 01:05:36.948 align:middle line:84%
That could be going
on here, but we

01:05:36.948 --> 01:05:38.810 align:middle line:84%
can check that that's
not what's going on.

01:05:38.810 --> 01:05:41.170 align:middle line:90%
So let's try to interpret this.

01:05:41.170 --> 01:05:46.170 align:middle line:84%
Well, we already have a
formula for the equilibrium

01:05:46.170 --> 01:05:47.687 align:middle line:90%
in the special uniform case.

01:05:47.687 --> 01:05:49.770 align:middle line:84%
So let's see if this agrees
with our answer there.

01:05:49.770 --> 01:05:52.550 align:middle line:84%
If it doesn't agree there,
then we're kind of in trouble.

01:05:52.550 --> 01:06:01.850 align:middle line:84%
So in the uniform 0, 1
case, well, what is g of x,

01:06:01.850 --> 01:06:03.290 align:middle line:90%
and what is G of v?

01:06:03.290 --> 01:06:05.990 align:middle line:90%


01:06:05.990 --> 01:06:07.830 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's remember
what g is. g

01:06:07.830 --> 01:06:14.750 align:middle line:84%
is the distribution of the
maximum value of my opponents.

01:06:14.750 --> 01:06:17.310 align:middle line:84%
But if there's only two of
us, then the maximum value

01:06:17.310 --> 01:06:19.450 align:middle line:84%
of my opponents is just
the value of my opponent.

01:06:19.450 --> 01:06:23.190 align:middle line:84%
So in the uniform 0,
1, with two bidders,

01:06:23.190 --> 01:06:24.850 align:middle line:90%
what is g of x going to be?

01:06:24.850 --> 01:06:30.510 align:middle line:90%


01:06:30.510 --> 01:06:33.790 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be the
density of the distribution

01:06:33.790 --> 01:06:35.390 align:middle line:90%
of my opponent's value.

01:06:35.390 --> 01:06:37.670 align:middle line:84%
And what is that density
in the uniform case?

01:06:37.670 --> 01:06:38.250 align:middle line:90%
Just 1.

01:06:38.250 --> 01:06:44.790 align:middle line:90%


01:06:44.790 --> 01:06:46.610 align:middle line:90%
And what is G of v going to be?

01:06:46.610 --> 01:06:50.210 align:middle line:90%


01:06:50.210 --> 01:06:53.370 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's the CDF of
the uniform distribution.

01:06:53.370 --> 01:06:56.250 align:middle line:84%
So that's just v.
The probability

01:06:56.250 --> 01:06:59.970 align:middle line:84%
that a uniform distribution
is below v is just v assuming

01:06:59.970 --> 01:07:03.130 align:middle line:90%
v is between 0 and 1.

01:07:03.130 --> 01:07:05.130 align:middle line:90%
So let's plug this in.

01:07:05.130 --> 01:07:10.190 align:middle line:84%
And I guess the final step
is v lower bar is equal to 0.

01:07:10.190 --> 01:07:13.210 align:middle line:84%
So these are all the
special restrictions

01:07:13.210 --> 01:07:16.010 align:middle line:84%
we get in this uniform
example with two players--

01:07:16.010 --> 01:07:19.450 align:middle line:90%
I should say two bidders.

01:07:19.450 --> 01:07:20.298 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:07:20.298 --> 01:07:22.590 align:middle line:84%
I guess we'll see in real
time if we've made a mistake.

01:07:22.590 --> 01:07:24.290 align:middle line:84%
I don't think we
have, but we'll see.

01:07:24.290 --> 01:07:41.146 align:middle line:84%
So we get the integral
from 0 to v of x over v dx.

01:07:41.146 --> 01:07:47.020 align:middle line:90%
x times 1 divided by v dx.

01:07:47.020 --> 01:07:51.400 align:middle line:84%
Well, that's just 1
over v is a constant.

01:07:51.400 --> 01:07:57.660 align:middle line:84%
We can take that out of the
integral from 0 to v of xdx.

01:07:57.660 --> 01:07:59.400 align:middle line:90%
What is the antiderivative of x?

01:07:59.400 --> 01:08:01.180 align:middle line:90%
It's just x squared over 2.

01:08:01.180 --> 01:08:08.180 align:middle line:84%
So we get v squared
over 2 times 1 over v.

01:08:08.180 --> 01:08:09.760 align:middle line:84%
And, phew, we
didn't get it wrong.

01:08:09.760 --> 01:08:10.560 align:middle line:90%
We got v over 2.

01:08:10.560 --> 01:08:11.660 align:middle line:90%
So that's good.

01:08:11.660 --> 01:08:14.020 align:middle line:84%
So this formula is consistent
with our guess in the very

01:08:14.020 --> 01:08:14.840 align:middle line:90%
special case.

01:08:14.840 --> 01:08:16.680 align:middle line:84%
But this is a much
more general formula.

01:08:16.680 --> 01:08:19.580 align:middle line:90%


01:08:19.580 --> 01:08:24.060 align:middle line:84%
Now, how can we
interpret this formula?

01:08:24.060 --> 01:08:27.260 align:middle line:84%
There's actually quite a
nice way of looking at this.

01:08:27.260 --> 01:08:30.378 align:middle line:84%
Does this fraction
remind you of anything?

01:08:30.378 --> 01:08:30.920 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

01:08:30.920 --> 01:08:39.740 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone see a way of
interpreting this integral

01:08:39.740 --> 01:08:41.859 align:middle line:84%
in terms of conditional
expectations?

01:08:41.859 --> 01:08:46.760 align:middle line:84%
So G of v is the probability
that all my opponent's

01:08:46.760 --> 01:08:50.240 align:middle line:84%
valuations are
below v. And g of x

01:08:50.240 --> 01:08:53.399 align:middle line:84%
is the density of the
distribution of the maximum

01:08:53.399 --> 01:08:55.018 align:middle line:90%
of my opponent's valuations.

01:08:55.018 --> 01:08:56.560 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out that
this is actually

01:08:56.560 --> 01:08:58.100 align:middle line:90%
a conditional expectation.

01:08:58.100 --> 01:08:59.920 align:middle line:84%
And, again, this is
a probability fact.

01:08:59.920 --> 01:09:01.279 align:middle line:90%
So it turns out--

01:09:01.279 --> 01:09:03.880 align:middle line:90%
what is this?

01:09:03.880 --> 01:09:13.399 align:middle line:84%
It's actually the
expectation of the max of v2

01:09:13.399 --> 01:09:16.720 align:middle line:84%
through vn conditional
on the event

01:09:16.720 --> 01:09:38.359 align:middle line:84%
that this maximum is
less than or equal to v.

01:09:38.359 --> 01:09:42.000 align:middle line:84%
So this gives us a nicely
interpretable formula.

01:09:42.000 --> 01:09:43.660 align:middle line:90%
What does this say?

01:09:43.660 --> 01:09:46.100 align:middle line:84%
How much do I bid
in the equilibrium

01:09:46.100 --> 01:09:49.340 align:middle line:84%
of the first-price auction
when my value is v?

01:09:49.340 --> 01:09:54.460 align:middle line:84%
What I'm actually bidding
is the expectation

01:09:54.460 --> 01:09:58.060 align:middle line:84%
of the highest value
of my opponents,

01:09:58.060 --> 01:10:00.340 align:middle line:84%
conditional on the
event that the highest

01:10:00.340 --> 01:10:04.740 align:middle line:84%
value of my opponents is
less than or equal to v,

01:10:04.740 --> 01:10:06.640 align:middle line:84%
less than or equal
to my true valuation.

01:10:06.640 --> 01:10:14.900 align:middle line:90%


01:10:14.900 --> 01:10:17.840 align:middle line:84%
And there's a nice
connection here.

01:10:17.840 --> 01:10:19.717 align:middle line:90%
Maybe we'll try to do it.

01:10:19.717 --> 01:10:21.300 align:middle line:84%
But let me pause,
see I feel like I've

01:10:21.300 --> 01:10:22.342 align:middle line:90%
lost people a little bit.

01:10:22.342 --> 01:10:24.085 align:middle line:90%
So any questions?

01:10:24.085 --> 01:10:25.460 align:middle line:84%
I know it's been
a little mathier

01:10:25.460 --> 01:10:26.502 align:middle line:90%
than we've normally done.

01:10:26.502 --> 01:10:27.480 align:middle line:90%
So any questions here?

01:10:27.480 --> 01:10:28.360 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, let's pause.

01:10:28.360 --> 01:10:29.620 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:10:29.620 --> 01:10:33.580 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So that the condition
is that your valuation is higher

01:10:33.580 --> 01:10:35.460 align:middle line:90%
than the valuation is like--

01:10:35.460 --> 01:10:38.080 align:middle line:84%
higher than the valuation
of the maximum opponent.

01:10:38.080 --> 01:10:40.678 align:middle line:90%
What if that's not true?

01:10:40.678 --> 01:10:42.720 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So you're asking
about this final step?

01:10:42.720 --> 01:10:45.960 align:middle line:84%
So let's understand right
here. v is just a number.

01:10:45.960 --> 01:10:48.640 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm writing
here is just a formula

01:10:48.640 --> 01:10:50.560 align:middle line:84%
for just something
that's defined

01:10:50.560 --> 01:10:53.440 align:middle line:84%
as a function of this number
v. So mathematically, all this

01:10:53.440 --> 01:10:56.320 align:middle line:84%
is is a very special
conditional expectation where

01:10:56.320 --> 01:10:58.560 align:middle line:90%
this is a random variable.

01:10:58.560 --> 01:11:00.100 align:middle line:90%
This is a random variable.

01:11:00.100 --> 01:11:02.880 align:middle line:84%
And I'm just looking at a very
special conditional expectation

01:11:02.880 --> 01:11:04.060 align:middle line:90%
of a random variable.

01:11:04.060 --> 01:11:08.880 align:middle line:84%
So it may be that their
valuations are lower than mine.

01:11:08.880 --> 01:11:11.380 align:middle line:84%
What we're just saying is it's
kind of a thought experiment.

01:11:11.380 --> 01:11:13.005 align:middle line:84%
It's saying how much
am I going to bid?

01:11:13.005 --> 01:11:15.960 align:middle line:84%
Well, one way I can
compute my equilibrium bid

01:11:15.960 --> 01:11:20.960 align:middle line:84%
is by computing this very
special conditional expectation.

01:11:20.960 --> 01:11:23.560 align:middle line:84%
But you're right that the
true value of my opponents

01:11:23.560 --> 01:11:25.800 align:middle line:84%
may be lower than v. This
is just kind of a formula

01:11:25.800 --> 01:11:27.720 align:middle line:90%
for what my bid will be.

01:11:27.720 --> 01:11:28.720 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:11:28.720 --> 01:11:30.600 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But it could also be
that the true value of your bid

01:11:30.600 --> 01:11:31.740 align:middle line:90%
is higher than v, right?

01:11:31.740 --> 01:11:32.120 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: It could be.

01:11:32.120 --> 01:11:32.680 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:11:32.680 --> 01:11:34.930 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But you're just
saying like you're basing it

01:11:34.930 --> 01:11:37.940 align:middle line:84%
off of the idea that if
everything were less than

01:11:37.940 --> 01:11:39.665 align:middle line:90%
or equal v, like [INAUDIBLE].

01:11:39.665 --> 01:11:42.040 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: I can just define
this conditional expectation.

01:11:42.040 --> 01:11:44.620 align:middle line:84%
So for now, I mean,
it's just a definition.

01:11:44.620 --> 01:11:48.648 align:middle line:84%
It's just a function of v, and
it's a number we can compute.

01:11:48.648 --> 01:11:50.940 align:middle line:84%
But it gives us kind of a
nice interpretation that I'll

01:11:50.940 --> 01:11:51.898 align:middle line:90%
go through in a second.

01:11:51.898 --> 01:11:53.260 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:11:53.260 --> 01:11:53.760 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:11:53.760 --> 01:11:54.360 align:middle line:90%
In the back.

01:11:54.360 --> 01:11:58.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can you explain one
more time why g of x over G of v

01:11:58.460 --> 01:11:59.195 align:middle line:90%
is the--

01:11:59.195 --> 01:11:59.820 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah.

01:11:59.820 --> 01:12:00.580 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:12:00.580 --> 01:12:02.955 align:middle line:84%
And this is something-- yeah,
if you haven't seen before.

01:12:02.955 --> 01:12:05.972 align:middle line:90%
So this is--

01:12:05.972 --> 01:12:08.412 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Wouldn't
it also be the x

01:12:08.412 --> 01:12:09.380 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Say it again.

01:12:09.380 --> 01:12:10.755 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Instead
of just g of x,

01:12:10.755 --> 01:12:13.660 align:middle line:84%
would it-- because x g
of x is an expectation.

01:12:13.660 --> 01:12:16.435 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: So this is an
unconditional expectation.

01:12:16.435 --> 01:12:18.060 align:middle line:84%
When I put the g over
v here it becomes

01:12:18.060 --> 01:12:19.500 align:middle line:90%
a conditional expectation.

01:12:19.500 --> 01:12:22.740 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I wonder
why x is [INAUDIBLE]

01:12:22.740 --> 01:12:24.020 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Oh, I guess--

01:12:24.020 --> 01:12:26.000 align:middle line:84%
so it's a question
of what we circle.

01:12:26.000 --> 01:12:28.660 align:middle line:84%
So I'm saying the
conditional expectation

01:12:28.660 --> 01:12:32.580 align:middle line:84%
can be expressed as x times
the conditional density.

01:12:32.580 --> 01:12:34.820 align:middle line:84%
So what I've circled here
is the conditional density.

01:12:34.820 --> 01:12:37.510 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
conditional density

01:12:37.510 --> 01:12:40.613 align:middle line:84%
given the event that
everyone else's value is

01:12:40.613 --> 01:12:41.530 align:middle line:90%
below a certain thing.

01:12:41.530 --> 01:12:43.710 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll just say,
what's the general property

01:12:43.710 --> 01:12:44.690 align:middle line:90%
I'm using here?

01:12:44.690 --> 01:12:48.990 align:middle line:90%


01:12:48.990 --> 01:12:52.470 align:middle line:84%
Let me go down here and
write this kind of math fact.

01:12:52.470 --> 01:12:57.230 align:middle line:90%


01:12:57.230 --> 01:13:08.710 align:middle line:84%
So if you have a random variable
X distributed with a density g,

01:13:08.710 --> 01:13:12.690 align:middle line:84%
then we can also compute
the conditional density.

01:13:12.690 --> 01:13:15.190 align:middle line:84%
So we can say what is
the conditional density

01:13:15.190 --> 01:13:22.350 align:middle line:84%
g of x given that X is
less than or equal to v

01:13:22.350 --> 01:13:29.310 align:middle line:84%
And that's defined to be g of
x divided by the probability

01:13:29.310 --> 01:13:32.430 align:middle line:84%
that X is less than or
equal to v, which is exactly

01:13:32.430 --> 01:13:40.210 align:middle line:90%
g of x times big G of v.

01:13:40.210 --> 01:13:44.410 align:middle line:84%
One way to think about this
is if I want to understand

01:13:44.410 --> 01:13:47.090 align:middle line:84%
the conditional probability,
or the conditional distribution

01:13:47.090 --> 01:13:51.210 align:middle line:84%
of x, given that x is below v,
well, it's still proportional

01:13:51.210 --> 01:13:53.330 align:middle line:84%
to the density, but
now it has to integrate

01:13:53.330 --> 01:13:56.030 align:middle line:90%
to 1 over this condition of n.

01:13:56.030 --> 01:13:59.850 align:middle line:84%
So conditional on my
opponent's value being below v,

01:13:59.850 --> 01:14:02.290 align:middle line:84%
the conditional density,
if I integrate it

01:14:02.290 --> 01:14:04.530 align:middle line:84%
from v lower bar to
v, better give me 1,

01:14:04.530 --> 01:14:07.010 align:middle line:84%
because that's how conditional
distributions work.

01:14:07.010 --> 01:14:10.490 align:middle line:84%
And if we do this integral from
v lower bar to v of g of x,

01:14:10.490 --> 01:14:12.890 align:middle line:84%
we exactly get big
G of v. And then

01:14:12.890 --> 01:14:14.790 align:middle line:84%
if we divide it by
big G of v, we get 1.

01:14:14.790 --> 01:14:16.850 align:middle line:84%
So this is just--
it's a normalization

01:14:16.850 --> 01:14:20.410 align:middle line:84%
to ensure that the distribution,
the conditional distribution,

01:14:20.410 --> 01:14:22.090 align:middle line:90%
integrates to 1.

01:14:22.090 --> 01:14:24.992 align:middle line:84%
But again, as I said, we're
using a little more probability.

01:14:24.992 --> 01:14:26.450 align:middle line:84%
This is maybe the
one lecture where

01:14:26.450 --> 01:14:28.810 align:middle line:84%
we're kind of leaning on some
of these official prereqs

01:14:28.810 --> 01:14:30.250 align:middle line:90%
that we haven't really enforced.

01:14:30.250 --> 01:14:34.450 align:middle line:84%
And yeah, some of these things
may take a little refresher.

01:14:34.450 --> 01:14:38.710 align:middle line:84%
But this is the general
fact that I'm using.

01:14:38.710 --> 01:14:41.990 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

01:14:41.990 --> 01:14:45.750 align:middle line:90%
So one final observation.

01:14:45.750 --> 01:14:47.770 align:middle line:84%
If we try to look at
this equation star--

01:14:47.770 --> 01:14:54.190 align:middle line:90%


01:14:54.190 --> 01:14:55.210 align:middle line:90%
let me go over here.

01:14:55.210 --> 01:15:14.150 align:middle line:90%


01:15:14.150 --> 01:15:20.270 align:middle line:84%
So we said that if my valuation
is v, this is how much I bid.

01:15:20.270 --> 01:15:24.470 align:middle line:84%
So if I win the good, the
amount that I'm going to pay

01:15:24.470 --> 01:15:28.510 align:middle line:84%
is exactly beta of v, which is
exactly this formula over here.

01:15:28.510 --> 01:15:31.850 align:middle line:84%
But let's just go through a
little thought experiment.

01:15:31.850 --> 01:15:33.590 align:middle line:90%
Let's suppose that we instead--

01:15:33.590 --> 01:15:37.250 align:middle line:90%


01:15:37.250 --> 01:15:38.890 align:middle line:84%
we did a second-price
auction instead

01:15:38.890 --> 01:15:40.110 align:middle line:90%
of a first-price auction.

01:15:40.110 --> 01:15:48.770 align:middle line:90%


01:15:48.770 --> 01:15:52.350 align:middle line:84%
Well, now everyone's
going to bid truthfully.

01:15:52.350 --> 01:16:07.210 align:middle line:90%


01:16:07.210 --> 01:16:16.933 align:middle line:84%
So let's say the profile of
valuations is v1 through vn.

01:16:16.933 --> 01:16:18.850 align:middle line:84%
If we're all bidding
truthfully, and we're now

01:16:18.850 --> 01:16:22.330 align:middle line:84%
in a second-price auction,
when is bidder 1 going to win?

01:16:22.330 --> 01:16:26.210 align:middle line:84%
What must be true about
v1 through vn for bidder 1

01:16:26.210 --> 01:16:26.990 align:middle line:90%
to win the item?

01:16:26.990 --> 01:16:27.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:16:27.490 --> 01:16:29.145 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: V1 is
the max [INAUDIBLE]

01:16:29.145 --> 01:16:29.770 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah.

01:16:29.770 --> 01:16:31.312 align:middle line:84%
So maybe another
way I'll say it is--

01:16:31.312 --> 01:16:36.830 align:middle line:84%
so bidder 1 wins if V1
is greater than or equal

01:16:36.830 --> 01:16:38.790 align:middle line:84%
to-- or maybe I'll
say greater than--

01:16:38.790 --> 01:16:46.390 align:middle line:84%
I'll say greater than or equal
to the max of v2 up to vn.

01:16:46.390 --> 01:16:48.650 align:middle line:84%
And if they win,
what do they pay?

01:16:48.650 --> 01:16:54.190 align:middle line:90%


01:16:54.190 --> 01:16:57.210 align:middle line:84%
How much does bidder 1 pay
if they win the auction?

01:16:57.210 --> 01:17:02.590 align:middle line:90%


01:17:02.590 --> 01:17:06.270 align:middle line:84%
Well, bidder 1 pays the
second-highest bid, right?

01:17:06.270 --> 01:17:08.910 align:middle line:84%
The second-highest bid is
going to be the highest

01:17:08.910 --> 01:17:10.670 align:middle line:90%
bid of his opponents.

01:17:10.670 --> 01:17:12.870 align:middle line:84%
But his opponents are
bidding truthfully,

01:17:12.870 --> 01:17:14.510 align:middle line:84%
so the second-highest
bid is exactly

01:17:14.510 --> 01:17:20.770 align:middle line:84%
going to be the
maximum of v2 up to vn.

01:17:20.770 --> 01:17:29.000 align:middle line:90%


01:17:29.000 --> 01:17:33.640 align:middle line:84%
So in a second-price
auction as opposed

01:17:33.640 --> 01:17:37.457 align:middle line:84%
to a first-price auction, the
amount that I pay is random.

01:17:37.457 --> 01:17:39.040 align:middle line:84%
I don't know how
much I'm going to pay

01:17:39.040 --> 01:17:41.960 align:middle line:84%
because it's going to depend on
the valuations of my opponents.

01:17:41.960 --> 01:17:47.040 align:middle line:84%
But the amount that I'm
going to pay is exactly this.

01:17:47.040 --> 01:17:50.200 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to pay
that exactly if I win.

01:17:50.200 --> 01:17:53.320 align:middle line:84%
So what is the expected
amount that I'm

01:17:53.320 --> 01:17:57.040 align:middle line:90%
going to pay given that I win?

01:17:57.040 --> 01:18:00.600 align:middle line:84%
It's exactly going to
be-- so suppose I win.

01:18:00.600 --> 01:18:03.080 align:middle line:84%
What is my conditional
expected payment?

01:18:03.080 --> 01:18:07.560 align:middle line:84%
It's exactly the expectation of
this, conditional on the event

01:18:07.560 --> 01:18:09.920 align:middle line:84%
that this is less
than or equal to v1.

01:18:09.920 --> 01:18:11.560 align:middle line:90%
Wait a second.

01:18:11.560 --> 01:18:14.320 align:middle line:84%
That's exactly the
formula we got over here.

01:18:14.320 --> 01:18:16.460 align:middle line:84%
So this is an expression
of revenue equivalence.

01:18:16.460 --> 01:18:19.680 align:middle line:84%
It says that in a
first-price auction

01:18:19.680 --> 01:18:22.840 align:middle line:84%
and a second-price auction,
the expected amount

01:18:22.840 --> 01:18:25.820 align:middle line:84%
that each type of bidder pays is
actually going to be the same.

01:18:25.820 --> 01:18:28.980 align:middle line:84%
Here, I bid B of v.
It's just one number,

01:18:28.980 --> 01:18:31.100 align:middle line:90%
and I always pay that if I win.

01:18:31.100 --> 01:18:34.082 align:middle line:84%
Here, how much I pay depends
on what other people bid.

01:18:34.082 --> 01:18:35.540 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what
I'm going to pay.

01:18:35.540 --> 01:18:37.748 align:middle line:84%
If other people have valuations
really close to mine,

01:18:37.748 --> 01:18:38.720 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to pay a lot.

01:18:38.720 --> 01:18:40.160 align:middle line:84%
If they all have
valuations really low,

01:18:40.160 --> 01:18:41.340 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to pay a little.

01:18:41.340 --> 01:18:43.250 align:middle line:84%
But the expected amount
that I'm going to pay

01:18:43.250 --> 01:18:45.500 align:middle line:84%
is actually going to be
exactly the same as the amount

01:18:45.500 --> 01:18:47.083 align:middle line:84%
that I pay in the
first-price auction.

01:18:47.083 --> 01:18:49.300 align:middle line:84%
And this is the first
indication of this revenue

01:18:49.300 --> 01:18:52.500 align:middle line:84%
equivalence fact, that
while the way that we bid

01:18:52.500 --> 01:18:55.500 align:middle line:84%
is going to be different, the
expected amount that each bidder

01:18:55.500 --> 01:18:56.920 align:middle line:90%
pays is going to be the same.

01:18:56.920 --> 01:18:59.080 align:middle line:84%
And for that reason, the
auctioneer's revenue,

01:18:59.080 --> 01:19:01.038 align:middle line:84%
expected revenue, is also
going to be the same.

01:19:01.038 --> 01:19:02.780 align:middle line:84%
So this is the first
hint at something

01:19:02.780 --> 01:19:05.060 align:middle line:84%
called revenue
equivalence, which

01:19:05.060 --> 01:19:07.340 align:middle line:84%
we saw in a special
example before,

01:19:07.340 --> 01:19:09.720 align:middle line:84%
and now we see is a much
more general phenomenon.

01:19:09.720 --> 01:19:12.020 align:middle line:84%
But we'll talk about that
a bit more next class.

01:19:12.020 --> 01:19:13.920 align:middle line:84%
I guess it's a
holiday on Tuesday,

01:19:13.920 --> 01:19:15.900 align:middle line:84%
so I guess I'll see
everyone next Thursday.

01:19:15.900 --> 01:19:18.000 align:middle line:84%
And I'm happy to stick
around with any questions.

01:19:18.000 --> 01:19:19.550 align:middle line:90%
Thanks.

01:19:19.550 --> 01:19:23.000 align:middle line:90%